      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>WOMEN AND Moron RACING.</p>
        <p>There are very few things which a woman cannot do, but there are 60/110 which a lady does not—or, in my opinion, should not—want to do, writes "Rutulan" in the Gentle- woman ; track racing on a motor-car, before a crowd of people, is one of these. Call me old-fashioned, prudish. or what you will, I am sure my opinion will be shared by many motorists of bol sexes. In this connection it is interesting to note that both the Royal Automobile Club and the &amp;offish Auto. mobile Club decided this year not to allow ladies to drive irr their long distance reliability trials; this in spite of the fact that in previous years ladies have driven, yes, and scored well too, in these trials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>DAINTY MUSLIN BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A DAINTY MUSLIN BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our sketch is a delightfully dainty and pretty little muslin blouse, quite smart and dressy enough to wear e a garden:party, wed- ding, or similar function in alliance with a well- made white cloth or serge skirt. The muslin of which this attractive little blouse is made is per- fectly plain, and extremely fine, soft, and trans- parent. The blouse itself is made in pinafore style, the shaped square-cut top being finished by a trimming compozcd of a band of Cluny in-</p>
        <p>sertion, a band of the mslin patterned with big embroidered spots, and an edging of Cluny lace. Beneath this yoke, as it were, the blouse is gathered all round the top; the fulness being allowed to pouch a little at the waist. In the square opening at the neck comes first a little tucked vest of the muslin, edged with a band of insertion, and then a simple chemisette of fine white Irish crochet. Pretty loose overslecves, laced and embroidered to match the blouse. open over underslceves of gauged muslin, which finish at the three-quarter length in minute .pleated</p>
        <p>••• ." )10:-N _ __, . •41.• ,- li•A 1,, ~ 1,,q4, -1 n ~. . • oiii,..- „...... C 11 \\, iI • ''' „ 11. ; i!:i ~ \ 1 . .:'`../1 MP il/ ! \ '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TEE TIMID SEX!</head>
        <p>TEE TIMID SEX!</p>
        <p>Once upon a time woman shrieked at a mouse; now, if she "shrieks," it is in order to effect a reform. AS a matter of fact, remarks the Sketch, it would be - grossly inaccurate to describe womenfolk as "timid." Now that the ladies compete in motor races at sixty miles an hour, go up, as a matter of course, in balloons and dirigibles, make raids on the House of Commons, asctnd the Shreckhorn, run tobog- gan races at St. Moritz, and even invite men to dine at their clubs, they cannot, in any fairness, ho classed among the shy and recluse main- malia. Moreover, every astute observer of the Human Comedy has long been aware that women have far more moral courage than men. What man, for instance, dares to dismiss (or even to rebuke) his cook without infinite trepi- dation? What man would have the courage to wear the masculine equivalent of a Directoire dress and a matinee hat of vast proportions?_ What male person would have the audacity to snub the right person with the supreme imperti- nence shown by his more courageous women- kind?- Man, too, is much more the slave of social ritual than woman, hence his heroic ad- herence to black-cloth coats and top-hats in the most torrid weather, and his performance of other uncomfortable rites which woman, with her larger latitude, would not consent to under- g). Some other epithet, it is obvious, /will have to be found for The Sex</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SMART WAS-RING 14'ROCK.</head>
        <p>A SMART WAS-RING 14'ROCK.</p>
        <p>Very smart and attractive is the practical little washing frock which forms the subject of our sketch. The material in which it is realhied is the fashionable "mattress," in this cas.e of dead white tone patterned with a big check in poPPY- red. At the neck the corsage is much hollowed out, di,:playing beneath a dainty little vest of fine tucked lawn. Round the top of the corsage collies a narrow band of plain white material, edged on either side with a line of machine. stitching. Below this the material is used with</p>
        <p>the check on the perpendicular. Then comes it shaped band of material usell on the crass, giv- ing somewhat the effect of a bertho, and just ap- pearing below this band the loose bodice' also cut with the chock on the straight. The sleeves are simple full affairs made with the check on the perpendicular, and are finished at the three- quarter length by stitched cuffs of the plain white material. The skirt is simple and quite plain Faro fcr the wide band of crossway mate- rialwhich is put on just about the -level of the knees. 'TuE QtEEN AND MME. REJANE. Mme. Rejanc was walking the other day along the paths which intersect the Elite Gardens at the Franco-British Exhibition. According to the Gentlewoman, a lady, elegant, full 'of grace, and closely veiled, passed her by, then turning sud- denly exclaimed, " Ah, Mme. Rejane! C'est rous! How do you do?" Mine. Wejane, beam- ing with smiles, made reply, as she•recognised in the veiled lady none other than Queen Alexandra.</p>
