      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECI£E.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECI£E.</p>
        <p>ISTEANSTERRT SHORTCAKE.—Mix with one pint of flour a teaspoonful of cream of tartar and a pinch of salt, then rub in two and a-half ounces .of lard or butter. Now dissolve half a level tea- spoonful of carbonate of soda in sufficient milk to form the flour -into a stiff dough, divide this into two parts and lay on a baking-tin. having 1 ornamented tne cages. wnen baked, cut on the upper pieces of crust, butter the inner sides ' lightly, and place a layer one inch thick of strawberries, slightly mashed and sweetened, be. tween the two crusts. Garnish the top with strawberries sprinkled with castor sugar, cut in aticea, and pcur thick erearn oyet., _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>A Memorial to Mrs. Oliphant.—The mernerial to Alrs. Oliihant, which was unveiled at Edinburgh by Mr..). M. Barrie, commemorates a very remarkable literary career, the industry of this distinguished Scottish writer being equalled only by -her versatility. Mrs. Oliphant published her first novel at the age of twenty-one, and two years later began to centribute to " Blacks o:As' Magazine," for which, from first to last, she wrote considerably more than a hundred articles. ller books would make a smell library in themselves for they numher more than 120, and comprise novels, essays, histories, works of travel and description. There are of course some parallels to such sersatility, es for example in the case of Goldsmith, whose necessities impelled him to prcduce poems, plays, histories, essays, and reviews, but it is Sc rcely possible that a success- , ful novelist or playwright should be eminent also as an historian, and the most ardent admirers of Mrs. Oliphant's work can hardly expect that all her books will live. 'the charm and beauty of the style will I certainly cause some of them to survive, and not only the people of Scotland, but all the British race, most remain interested in the life of one who was overtaken by many grievous misfortunes and mettLem with sur- passing courage. iss Marie Corelli's New - M istovel.—August would not be August without I new novel by Miss Marie Corelli, and it is stated that her forthcoming story will be entitled " Holy Orders :—The Tragedy of a Quiet Life." Miss Corelli always makes a point of with- holding the.title of her novels until near publication, but she satisfies the curiosity of her countless readers on this occasion by letting so much be known, and it is understood that her new story is concerned with religiomand the virtues and vices of society. Miss Corelli, who is of mingled Italian and Scottish extrac- tion was educated in a French convent, and it is said that she knew Shakespeare almost by heart before she was in her teens. Eer first volume " A Romance of Two Worlds " was published in ISSG, when she was twenty-two years of age. The book was at once a great success and from that time no woman writer of tisis or any other period, had has such an enormous public as Miss Corelli commands. Fancy Price for Fancy Dogs.—There can be few mere prontable occupations than the breeding of fashionable "sleeve dogs." Sometimes it is one kind of dog, and sometimes another that is all the rage, but the Society woman must have a toy dog of some sort, and will pay any price for her fancy. The Pekinese is not quite SO fashionable as it used to be, but it has its own separate Association, and at its first show the other day there was an animal,valired by its owner at 2.3,000 or twenty times or more its weight in gold. So delicate are these toy dogs, that with numbeis of them life bangs only by a thread, and there are hundreds of fashionable women to whom it is nothing unusual to sit up day and night to nurse their pets through a crisis in their lives. It is the unhappy way %Melt these pets have of departing this file just when they might become as valuable as a racehorse, that seems to be the</p>
        <p>only drawback to the fashional,le—dog-breeding business. Forty or fifty guineas is the average price of a good Pekinese, if not more than 4lhs. or stbs. in weight, although two or threetitnes as much is nothing unusuil for those with special points, and a little smaller in weight and size. Te Poor Unprotected Bach elor.—Preak legislative proposals are common enough in the various American States, but one of more than usual interest seems to have been proposed by Representative George Glenn,in the State of Georgia. Mr. Glenn is a married roan, and having been on a visit to various summer resorts, has introduced a Bill which provides that if any woman, maid or widow shall betray into matrimony any moo-</p>
        <p>cent male citizen by means of cosmetics,scents,powders, open-work blouses, hosiery, or various other blandish- ments and artful practices, the marriage may le tleclared null and void. Here, says the" mere we have something like justice for the bachelor, but it is not flattering to the Georgia women to suggest that he is need of such protection. It is more than probable that they will have a word to say to Mr. Representative Glenn, if indeed that gentleman does not meet with the fate which Mr. Asquith pictured the other day as possibly his own latter end, that of being torn to pieces by wild women. The Dear Meat " Scare."—Happily the dear meat "scare" shows signs of fizzling out long before the arrival of winter, %erten there is a larger prtbfio demand for, butchers' supplies. To a great extent people can testily go without meat in the summer time, a fact which those who engineered the "scare" do not appear to have taken sufficiently into account or perhaps they were prevented from doing so by force of circumstances. The public is accustomed to being periodically squeezed by the forcing up of prices, on the smallest pretence. Last year it was bread, this year meat, and although prices are quickly put up, the coming down again is generally a very slow process. Prices in the wholesale meat trade are said to have returned to their normal level, but it will probably be some time before those of the butcher follow suit.</p>
        <p>Jewelled Draperies.—Of the private balls of the past London season, that given last week by the Duke and Duchess of Westminster at Grosvenor House, was the most brilliant, the King, Queen, many 'loyalties, nod over 1,000 guests being present. Tha ballroom, as may be imagined#•presented a magnificent display of beauti- ful dresses and jewelm, and a striking feature of the jewellers' art was introduced by one or two of the guests, in the form of a varnitare (le earsage,or shoulder drapery of large net, composed of fine diamonds, The gems %tore secured to an all but invisible gold thread, and this costly fabric depending some five or-six inches from the shoulders, was secured back and front with diamond cords and tassels. Needless to say the effect of the jewelled drapery was dazzling in the extreme,and conspicuous even in the blaze of jewels nhich permeates the ballrooms of fashionable society. Cream and Ivory Hues for Middle Age.—Only a very few years ago, no woman past thirty would have dared I to wear white, but we have advanced greatly in the art ' of dress since those days, and now realise that whereas colours are often anathema to a faded complexion, I cream and ivory white help to tono down sallowness and give grace to hair that is turning grey. Of course no woman past her youth should indulge in dead cold white, cream and ivory are her tones, and further, she should see to it when choosing a gown of this descrip- tion, that a little real old lace is disposed about the bodice.</p>
        <p>Vegetables in our Hats.—One is accnstomed to the fashionable milliner choosing gr,pes, plums, currants, and cherries for the adornment of her " creations," but when tomatoes, both green and red, are called into requisition for this purpose one feels puzzled. by cot a cluster of carrots, or a bunch of leeks ? Vet in the up-to-date hat the tomato flourishes, and one or two miniature specimens axe frequently being pinned into the ruffle or scarf, to help carry out the scheme. Truly, the freaks on the part of Dame Fashion are past comprehension.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
