      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, July 25, 1908,</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, July 25, 1908,</p>
        <p>Plio:C GRAPHIC APPARAWS AND MATERIALS OF EVERY DESCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>I.AliGebT STOCK Is TOTS. HAROLD SMITH, PHLIOGRAPIi ICJ CIIIBMIST, BUIEGIS ST., NVAILIIINGL(.I7.</p>
        <p>(Copyrigh(.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>INSANITY AND lIEREDITY.</p>
        <p>Professor Wagner von Jauregg, in the course of a lecture' before the Austrian Criminal Lawyers' Association, has afforded great hope in a direction where it is often sorely needed. He argued that, if a person has ' insanity in the family" he is not neces- sarily liable to suffer from hereditary mental disease. He presented statistics shewing that among the . desc.kendants of madmen 59 per cent, are mentally sound. This proves conclusively, he said, that the descendants of madmen are not necessarily foredoomed, is is popularly supposed, '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>, ; HOW CATERPILLARS GET COLOUR.</head>
        <p>, ; HOW CATERPILLARS GET COLOUR.</p>
        <p>. , i Experiments shew that the yellow and 1 1 green colours „possessed by certain cater- pillars are due to colouring, matter derived from the food, and passed through the blood of the spinners. By impregnating leaves with artificial colours the experimenters caused ' some species of caterpillars to produce silk . of bright orange-yellow and fine rose hues. ,By the aid of the spectroscope, an instru- ment used for making and examining the • coloured rays of which lighL. is composed, the presence and nature of coloured pigments in the blood of the little animals were established. - SEARCHING FOR A LUNAR ATMOSPHERE An official of the naval observattiry at Washington describes one of the methods of testing the question of the existence of a per. eeptible atmosphere on the moon. This is by the observation of a: star at the instant when it disappears.; or ,emerges from behind the moon. If there were a lunar atmosphere it should produce some effect on the appearance of the star. No such effect lice ever been ob- served. Photographs of stars, made es they were about to disappear behind the lunar disc', shew no indications 'of change in the in- tensity of the image, such as would be ex- pected to occur if the moon had a perceptible atmosphere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>COLLIERIES UNDER THE SEA.</head>
        <p>COLLIERIES UNDER THE SEA.</p>
        <p>At Cape Breton there are immense cul- lieries being worked under the ocean. The submarine mines cover 1,000 acres, and are being, increased steadily. The mines are en- tered at the shore, and the operators follow the rein beneath, the water for more than a mile. It might be expected that tile weight of the water would force its way into the mine. The bed of the ocean is as tight as a cement cistern. A sort of fircelay lines the submarine roof of the mine, and the sedi- ment above this is held in place and ,packed down by the water pressure until there is not a crevice nor a drop of water from overhead.</p>
        <p>FREEZING EGGS TO PRESERVE THEM.</p>
        <p>A comparative test of the American plan of freezing eggs to preserve them, with the method of treating with lime, has been made, by a scientific French experimenter. The refrigeration process is pronounced hygieni- cally superior. At low temperature, even when continued several months. there is no perceptible change in the taste or properties of the egg. The yolk retains its position in- stead of falling to the lower end, as in lime preservation, and the only change in appear- ance is a little greater wateriness. It is neces- sary, however, to keep the temperature about 2deg. Fahr. below freezing-point, with the hygrometer (an instrument used for measuring moisture in . the atmosphere), as near ',Nev. as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>-STRENGTH OF RINGS.</head>
        <p>-STRENGTH OF RINGS.</p>
        <p>Some elaborate calculations, backed by ex- periments, have been made to determine the breaking strength of rings. It appears that A ring of ductile metal, like malleable iron, will be pulled out into the form of a long link before it breaks, and that the ultimate strength of the ring is virtually independent of its diameter. Fracture finally occurs as the result of almost pure tension, and the re- sistance to breaking is a little less then twice that a rod of the same cross-section sub- jected to a straight pull. As the ring in- creases in diameter there appears to be a slight approach towards equality, with double the strength of a bar. Thus a 3in. ring, made of in. iron, broke at 14 tons; a 4in. ring at 19.9 tons, and it '6in. ring at 20 tons, the strength of a bar of the same metal being 101 tons.</p>
        <p>CLASSES OF BRAIN POWER.</p>
        <p>Famous brains are of four kinds, states Ilausemann. The lowest group contains the minds that are stimulated greatly by alcohol, tea, and other drugs, and by impressions de- rived from the senses;, the second group con- tains the infant prodigies, whose intellectual powers wane in middle age. In the third group are contained the diseased cases usually terminating in insanity • the fourth and highest group is that of ;rue geniuses, whose powers remain unimpaired until old age. Spitzka has come to the conclusion that men eminent in exact sciences like astro- nomy and mathematics have the greatest ave- rage brain weight. Next come the men of action, including statesmen and artists, and after these come the biologists, geologists, and other repfesentatives of the descriptive sciences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>INSECTS THAT DESTROY METAL.</head>
        <p>INSECTS THAT DESTROY METAL.</p>
        <p>The astonishing feet that in the Vienna mint the leaden walls of a reservoir contain- ing sulphuric acid, although forty-three milli- metres, or about I.7in. thick, were eaten through by an insect; that the leaden gas pipe in a café was also damaged in like man- ner, and that also in the sulphuric acid fac- tory in Nussdorf the wall of the lead cham- ber was found to contain defects from the same cause, has attracted attention to the damages done by insects both to wood and to metal. Such damages are due, says the Scien- tific American, to a sort of wood wasp, of which there are many sorts in Central Europe. The largest of these, the black and yellow giant wood wasp, resembles the true wasp, which is so feared by reason of its sting; but close observation shews it to be very different. Its breast and belly are joined by a wide connecting piece, whereas .in the ease of the real wasp the "waist" is prover- bially small. On the under side of the elon- gated belly the female has a</p>
        <p>VERY HARD BORING DEVICE, about nineteen millimetres (in) long, black, and fluted, and which lies in its sheath. Ordi- narily. this borer is directed backwards; but when in use it is turned about its base, so as to make a considerable angle the axis of the body, and is used like a rat-tail file until it makes a hole about eighteen milimetres (Olin.) deep in the wood, which it usually chooses to perforate. The egg which the female lays in the wood develops into a caterpillar-like creature with six short legs, and without eyes. (What would it do with eyes? About two years—during i almost its en- tire life—it,lives in the wood, n perfect (lark- ness!) With its sharp, hard jaws it bites in the trunk of the tree tubular channels, which increase in diameter as it grows larger. It swallows the wood which it gnaws off, digest- ing the nutritious portions and discharging the rest in a meal-like form. For two years it</p>
        <p>EATS ITS WAY FORWARD in this manner. In the third year the insect creeps out, biting with its jaws through the thin wall which separates it from the outer world, leaving the home of its child- hood to enter upon a short life in freedom. Should a tree trunk that hae been per- forated by such a wasp, and in which an egg has been laid, be employed when insuffici- ently seasoned for building purposes, it may happen that some _day the insect, which has been two years working ite way through the piece, will suddenly ;appear in the building. If a piece of such timber which contains a larva is surcounded by a leaden plate, the in- &amp;..et will not stop at this, but</p>
        <p>WILL BITE ITS WAY THROT.IOII just as though it were of wood. Almost more wonderful are the perform. ances of the boring cricket. Although this is a dwarf compared with the wasp, it has been able in Rochelle to gnaw through the leaden roof of a building and to make holes 14mm. (0.55 in. thep and 4nena. (0.16 iii. in diameter in printers' stereotype plates, despite the fact that by reason of the ,antimony in such plates they are much harder than lead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Veronica.</p>
        <p>THE PHOTOGRAPHIC CONVENTION.</p>
        <p>The experiment of holding the Photographic Con- vention of the United Kingdom in a foreign country has resulted in a great success front every point pf view save one, the local membership was very small. The ancient cities of Brussels, Ghent, Antwerp and Mechlin, with their caltles, cathedrals, monasteries and other fine old building 3 provided a bewildering wealth of camera-material. Sir. E. Cecil Hertalet, the Presi- dent, with Commandant Van Bever, Mr.Vanderkindere, and the other members cf the local committee were admirably backed by the local authorities of Ghent, Antwerp, and Mechlin in their efforts to interest their two hundred and fifty visitors. Almost all the public buildings of interest were open, free of charge, to wearers of the Convention badge, and in some cases they were allowed a couple of hours for photographing while the general public was excluded. NEXT YEAR'S PHOTOGRAPHIC CONVENTION</p>
        <p>For next year, the place of meeting is to be Canter- bury, and the President-elect is Mr. fl. Snowden Ward F.R.P.S., a resident in Kent, and the editor of the Photographic Monthly. The county which has been acclaimed as the garden of England (Afars great attract- Lions, and some of those Conventiovers who object that the excursions usually provide "too much architecture" will be pleased to know that the excursions next year, are likely to include a large amount of beautiful landscape and river scenery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPIIING;SIIMMER SPORTS.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPIIING;SIIMMER SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The Olympic games have probably given more impetus to the photographing of sport than anything else which has occurred in recent years. All over the country, athletic, agricultural, and other meetings are offering opportunities for interesting snap-shot record work, and a few suggestions and cautions may save some of my readers from preliminary failures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>APPARATUS AND MATERIALS.</head>
        <p>APPARATUS AND MATERIALS.</p>
        <p>Undoubtedly the best camera for sports work is a "reflex," with its full-sized finder in which the subject can be watched right up to the moment of exposure. And the best shutter is the focal-plane, which gives much more " efficiency "at any given speed than does the shutter on the lens ; and which can be worked at higher speeds. The lens should cover the plate sharply with an aperture of F 5. Gor FG. And the plates should be very rapid, but not ultra-rapid. It is important also that the plates should be "hacked "if the very best-11'3ga- tives are desired. For development, use any energetic developer, but keep it dilute and well restrained. It may be best used in a developing tank, and prcbably nothing is Letter for this impose than the glycin " stand " developer which I have given already in this column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SELECTION OF SUBJECT.</head>
        <p>SELECTION OF SUBJECT.</p>
        <p>Generally speaking,sports pictures must necessarily be under-exposed, and the safe rule will be to give the 41owcst shutter exposure that will be quick enough to prevent blurring from motion. For effective pictures the contestants should be as large as possible on the plate, and should be as well grouped as possible. If the light is poor and you have not a focal-plane shutter, good, interesting pictures of the straining contestants on the line just before the start, can often be made. If all things are favourable, the winning-post is the most interesting place. Take a position where the lighted side of the contestants Is towards you, rather than one with the sun shining on the lens • and be careful about the background against N 1 hid; the contestants are shown. One often sees a blurred mass of spectators in the background, I% here a change of positicn would have shewn the competors with the grass of the course behind them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>EXPOSURE.</head>
        <p>EXPOSURE.</p>
        <p>As a rough guide to the speed at uhich the shutter should be bet, I quote the following table from "llapd- camera work "by Me. Walter Kiting. This applies to objects at a distance about one hundred times the focus of the lens, that is, for a lens of six inches focus, objects 50 Let distant. You may practically reckon then when the subject is at half the distance. or 25 feet. not more than half the exposure can be allowed, and that when it is much nearer, say at 12 feet, it is certainly not safe to give more than about one-fourth the exposure here indicated. This means that for exciting finishes of races, am hen the camera is close to the winning post, a focal-plane shutter working at one-eight-hundredth or one-thousandth of a second should be used.</p>
        <p>At right Towards angles to camera. camera. SEC. Men walking, children playing, &amp;e. 1-40th 1-120th Cycling, ordinary -100th 1-300th Pony and trap trotting l-noth 1-300th Man running a race and jumping 1-150th 1-450th Cycle racing l-200th 1-600th Horses galloping l-200th 1-600th . _ .</p>
        <p>- • - Trains -200th 1-600th SELLING SPORTS PICTURES. Many earnest amateurs waste a good deal of money in sending snap-shot prints of local sporting events to the London illustrated papers. There are hundreds of such events every week in summer, and although their local interest is great the country as a whole cares nothing about them. Even a first-cla39 county cricket match is of insufficient interest for the London press unless it is a critical game in the championship,or some important player achieves some great feat. If a bats- man makes his tenth " century " for the season, people like to see his portrait ; and if it can show him making the decisive stroke, so much the better. For things of local interest, local sale must be sought, and often the bromide post-card, at twopence, if produced very quickly, can be made to earn many honest pennies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FOR THE SAKE OF</head>
        <p>FOR THE SAKE OF</p>
        <p>JOHN.</p>
        <p>BY KATHARINE S. MACQUOID, luthor of "The Story of Lois," "A Ward of tho rng," "At the Red Glovo," "In the Volcanio Mel," "Miss Ryon of Eyoncourt," "The Prince's Whim," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>It W 9.3 a lovely spring day. The trees in Hyde Park wore those powdery green tints which seem too beautiful for earth; they themselves appear to hold the same opinion, so fugitive are they in their visit. The sky had a strain of purple in its blue. The hum of voices and of traffic told that human life mingled with this lovely nature, and was close at hand. _ _ .</p>
        <p>A man who had entered the Park at Queen's- gate, when he was half-way acros3, stopped and greeted another. The second man had come in by the Marble Arch. He was below middle height, had a good sure head, and a singularly clever face. The first man was tall and welfmade. He looked clever, also handsome, but his face was overcast by an expression of deep anxiety. \ "Hallo! What's up ?'7 said the shorter man. "Nothing wrong with your people, I hope?" "I believe not. I'm now going to sea them," the tall man answered. He paused, then added abruptly: "I don't suppose you ever suffer from worries, Greer?" He looked so wistful that his friend was puzzled. What could John Merton have to worry over—a fellow, who, after a successful school careep, had taken a a,'.mble first at Oxford arid who at thirty was already a rising barrister? Greer smiled as he answered: "..1 don't see what l've got to worry about. I have not any responsi- bilities. That's the best of a public office in my branch of the service; in the evening one leaves care behind."</p>
        <p>..... "The sort of worry I mean is personal,' comes nearer home." The anxious look again settled on Merton's face. • .</p>
        <p>_ Greer shrugged his shoulders. "Yon are down in the mouth, Jack. Are you free this evening ? Yes ? All right, then well dine at the club and go to the Haymarket." They nodded and parted. As John Merton left the Park and crossed the road his lips became compressed and his head drooped forward. Presently, as he turned out of the Edgware-road into a street of neat-looking houses, he raised his bead andlorced a smile. By the time he reached the middle of the street and stopped before a house with a brightly-kept door, and gay boxes of spring 'flowers in the...windows, he looked lighthearted and happy, quite another man. "Your mother's out, I regret to say." While ke spoke, Mr. Merton senior, tried to keep his square, squat body between his son and a man who stood behind him. Taking this man by the arm he pushed him through the doorway into a room behind, saying over his shoulder to his son: "I'll be back directly." John Merton felt nauseated. This was the third time he had found that greasy, drunken-looking vagabond with his father. To-day before the door could be closed behind the seeming vagabond a hoarse, whining voice cried out: "What d'ye mean? Lernme go, I tell yer I I'm ciune for, the cash, so hand it over, or I'll—" The' sounds stopped as though the utterer had been suddenly gagged. A few minutes later Mr. Merton came back.</p>
        <p>..... . "Your mother will greatly regret to have missed yon," ho said, carelessly.' I "Father," John spoke quickly, "you had much better let me rid you of that fellow. Who is he? I suspect he tries to get money out of you." The difference in appearance between father and son was very striking. Something in the father's crouching figure betpoke subservience. There was a cringeing deference in his manner as if he always lived with his superiors. It totally lacked inde- pendence. His manner to his son made it difficult to believe he could be father to, so refined and highly cultivated a person as John Merton. The older man softly rubbed his hands together as he answered: "Very kind of you, I'm sure, John. But he's quite harmless, though seldom sober. I fear one must not notice what he says. I knew him before he took to these courses, and now and again I give him a little help. Nothing to make you uneasy, dear boy, be sure of that. Will you not sit down and wait till your mother comes in?" John Merton- was thoroughly upset. He had tried in vain to pooh-pooh the anxious presenti- ments of the last few days. Now the words he had overheard justified them. The man had threatened. John saw, however, that his father did not intend to satisfy his suspicions; he niust therefore find out this mystery for himself.</p>
        <p>Th friends frequently saw each other, and Greer noted that Merton's anxious look had not left him, it was the more noticeable because John's simple, cheerful manner had been the charm that first attracted his friend to him ;it had led to the firm friendship new established between them. One chilly, lovering afternoon Greer found him- self in •a narrow street in Holborn. He had been ransacking an old stall in search of a book he wanted, and at the corner of a dirty alley he met John Merton.</p>
        <p>_ Merton looked haggard and pale, but seemed delta-Med to meet his friend.</p>
        <p>"You! Just the man I want ; that is, if you'll be good enough to undertake my business." "With the greatest pleasure, old chap, if I can do it."</p>
        <p>"I'm sure you can. You often say you were born to be a detective. My case is not savoury, but 1 shall worry myself till it is taken in hand. At the same time, l've strong reasons against employ- ing a professional detective." Greer looked about him. The street in which they stood, and the alley Merton had come from, were both empty. "You can perhaps tell me here as well as any- where' to one seems Co be about." "And it has the advantage of being dose to the place I want you to visit," Merton answered. He took his friend's arm and led him back to the grimy little alley he had come from. In the dingy street, litter lay here and there in front of the houses. Merton pointed to a door some way down. Something was fastened to its knocker.</p>
        <p>"Do you see the door with a skewer of cat's nont under the knocker ? " •</p>
        <p>Greer nodded. "I'll take the cat's meat for granted. " "That door takes one into a yard with several doors leading out of it. There's a woman washing inside ; she'll tell you in Which house Old Gregory' is to he found. He imposes on my people: I've watched him and tracked him to this place, bat I don't want to interview him myself at first."</p>
        <p>"I see, you want me to tackle him? Tell me what I'm to say ?"</p>
        <p>"It's like this. I feel convinced the fellow's a rascal. Ile gets money out of my father under false pretences, and shat 'he gets he drinks. I don't believe in threats with a drunkard; I want to get rid of him. I'll pay his passage out to Australia or New Zealand ; for as long as he's over here I feel sure he'll try to -victimise my people.. You know how they most have pinched themselves for my sake, and now I can hardly persuade them to tuttept help from Inc. They say they don't need it.; I can't see them plundered. Besides, there's the look of the thing; it is not respectable to have h tnan like that calling at their house. I want to find out why he does it—what hold he says he has on my father." As he said the words Merton shrank into him- self. He wished to recall them. .Wa'i there really some shameful secret? And was he exposing it to Greer? And then, shocked at his own doubts, he told himself that his father was simple and upright in all his doings; lit.. could not have sacret dealings to be ashamed of. lie might be weakly generous, but it was impolsible that he had anything to hide. Greer was too careless and lighthearted to Share his friend's suspicions. • "I suppose, if necessary, lam to promise him money? These people are usually greedy." "Yes, I want to get him away, so r must pay his expenses and all that. If he's sulky; you'll have to humour him. I want to get rid of the fellow as soon as possible. I leave it in your hands to settle the best way you can. If he consents to leave the country I'll allow him something to live on. I don't fancy he'll need it long." "Where shall I find you after I've seen him?" Greer asked.</p>
        <p>"In my chambers. Thank you very much for helping me." •</p>
        <p>Greer opened the door with the cat's meat under the knocker, and found himself in a small court with four houses in it. One of the doors stood</p>
        <p>open,shewing, within, a Imp wash-tub and a cloud of steam, out of which peered the skinny face of a woman.</p>
        <p>seeing a gentleman in the court, she began to wipe her arms with her apron, then she pushed her tangled hair back from her eyes. .</p>
        <p>Greer asked for "Old Gregory.; She Pointed to the door on the right, and told him to go up till he came to the "top floor back." _ .</p>
        <p>"'Taint what you may call light up there, but you ketch fast hold of the stair-rail and go up as far as it takes you, an' you'll come out opposite."</p>
        <p>Greer followed the washerwoman's instructions. The darkness did not trouble him so much as the fetid atmosphere did. In one place the staircase- rail had been broken away and he nearly fell over into the darkness below. When he recovered him- self he found he had reached the top floor. A cough sounded close by. He knocked at a door opposite the stairhead. A i loud voice said "Come n" ; and then, before Greer found the handle, he again heard the hoarse cough and tlie door was opened from within. • A big figure so filled up the doorway that very little light came on to the landing. "What d'ye want?" the hoarse voice said. "What's yer bizness ?Is "I have business with a person named Gregory," Greer answered. "Are you he?" "My name's Gregory; but what your name and yer bizness ?" Greer hesitated. The insolent manner and blokted face Were not reassuring. It would be advisable, the young fellow thought, to keep the whip-hand over this man. "The business I come about is to Mr. Gregory'a advantage," he said, slowly; "but first I must be sure I've found the right person. Having ascer- tained that, I cannot discuss my business with him on an open staircase." "That's so, is it? Come your ways. I'm Gregory right enough. Who the deuce are you with in'name so pat ? " "My name is David. I'm employed by a benevolent person who is anxious to befriend you— that is, if you are willing to accept his help ? The big fellow could hardly wait till Greer ended. "Come yet ways," he croaked. But his manner was more, civil, his loose lips tightened as they spread in a grin across his face. Hurrying into the low room he pushed forward a rickety chair for his guest. There were two other chairs, but this one, covered with Mack horsehair, a good deal of which was now deficient, stood more firmly on its legs than the others did. The deal table was stained and greasy-looking. On it stood a green tobacco-jar, a glass, and an empty bottle. On the shelf above the rusty grate were pipes and a shabby pack of cards. An iron bedstead with scadty bedding stood in one corner ;,the rest of the room was bare, except for some empty bottles in a corner. Gregory did not ,trUst the other chairs. He Placed himself on the table, • then he looked suspiciously at hi 3 visitor. "Yer not wantin' me to take any dirty job in hand, are yet, now? If so, ye've come to the wrong shop, had yet journey for nothin'. I'm a gentleman, I tell yet; things have gone agin' me." He stared in so vacant and helpless a way that Greer felt sure he was not sober. All at once the man began to whimper: "See here, Mr. David, it ain't no fault of mine. I've lost my friends, so I've had t'o part from my things. I'm a gentleman born; that's to say, my father was. He went and married a fine lady, but he let my mother die in a horspital." Here he choked and coughed and spat. - "You have some friends left ? " Greer said. "They help you now and then, eh ? " Afterwards he wondered what induced him to say this; it wrought a striking change in the man before him.</p>
        <p>Gregory sat upright. The drunken leer left his lips; his bleared, pale eyes darkened with anger. "Friends you call ern? That's all you know. If you come from 'em you can make yourself scarce. They'w harmed me enough. To the deuce with such friends I But for them I'd live like a gentleman, and they know it. Friends I Liars and plunderers are too good names for them I" Greer was disgusted. Then be told himself the man was not sober, and that he must execute his friend's commission.</p>
        <p>"You'd like a change from this life, would you not?" _ _ _</p>
        <p>He looked steadfastly at the shifty face. Gregory's eyes stared ivonderingly at him, and then a sudden exciteislent shewed in them. • "You're meanin' to tell me somethin' good, ain't yet now? Yer throat's gettiu' dry? I know the feelin'. Worse luck, I'd drained the last drop before you came." Greer went on slowly, heedless of this hint. "When I say a change I mean you might in:prove on these lodgings, might you not ?" i Gregory shook his head and began to whine again. "They ain't what they was. .'Taint no fault of mine. Every stick's had to go." He bent forward till his red, swollen nose almost touched Greer's daintily - trimmed whiskers. Then in a hoarse, greasy whisper: " Don't mind if I tell yer the story? Why not ! That friend, as you call him, was no ketter than a servant to my father. The lowest clerk in the office, I reckon. Between ourselves, that was why the old gent put confidence in him." He winked. "You see, this clerk warn' t likely to be invited to the house, the wife being a fine lady: lay poor mother not having been treated to a wedding ring. Well, my mother died early, and father followed soot; but he told me he'd put away money in trust for me. I was sent to school, and I was to be placed in life when the time came. See, here, what was 1 sayin' P " He stopped,.,.Tubbed his chin, and looked foolish.</p>
        <p>1, When he spoke again he was at times incoherent he stuttered and wandered. Only after repeated j questioning was Greer able to make out a connected I meaning from his tipsy companion's words. j: At last the story began to take shape. David Greer shivered with disgust, and repeated Merton's offer of assistance. -</p>
        <p>- Gregory turned on him with scoffing laughter. "Why should Ig) abroad ? Not I. Mighty well to say I'll be provided for! Who's tc warrant that, Mr. David ? I'm a gentleman, and I have money by right. I've got the old man and his wife under my thumb; they're my living. Bless yer heart, they daren't deny me. Id go at once to the son; he's a swell, I expect, but I can't find him out."</p>
        <p>He began to stammer again. Greer found a! chance of speaking. "You'll get into trouble, my man. This sort ot thing can't go on. You'll be given in charge foi " Gregory stared, than laughed. "Give me in char;m tor takin' my own? I say my father left me that money, and if I was greedy I'd take it all. Listen here. That pair of cheato used to be as poor as rats. How do you suppose they Managed to send their precious son to, school and college? Why, at my expense. D'ye hear At my expense. That fine gent'll hear of it some! day when I can get hold of the right man, And ]; went on thinkin' the father'd sought me out and give me relief from charity! Good Lord!" Greer had risen. "If you're Wise, you'll take my advice and gc abroad. Australia or New Zealand, you know.! you'll have a sum paid you regularly, more than you get now. You've no legal claim on these people, I you knoW." Oregory stared. He rose, but had to steady himself against the table. I "Look at here, young, man. If I'm not in my righti why don't lie refuse to pay? She said at first they was old friends of my father's; and they , wished to help his son. Payment was Made I tuarterly then. It was just a chance I learnt! who the men really was, and the place he'd filled.' The chap that told me said he'd wondered how the follow'-d contrived to do what be had for his son. Well, Mr. David, I'm not a fool. I soon put that and that together and found out how it was. I get help now when I want it. I don't wait for no quarterly payments." Greer stood a nininent, he was stupefied; then mechanically lie went out of the room and down the shaky st airs. By the time he reached the last flight, the whole horror broke on him. A force, against which all I inward resistance was powerless, told him that the story was true—the tragedy of his friend's life. Greer had never loved anyone as he loved John! Merton. Evil seemed to have passed by his pure, I high-toned friend, without venturing to touch him, and yet if this horrible tale were true Merton was ' simply the product of evil.. Omer breathed more freely when he came out ' into the court. He stood still there thinking. Ji)lin had said lie would wait fo; him in his chambers. How could he face him? He could not go to him with this tale • he would not. His i friend need never know thahe had seen Gregory. I He opened the door leading from the court to the street, and started bacit. cuing him stood John Merton.</p>
        <p>"Well?" 4erton said, eagerly. you've done?" Greer stood a moment silent; but his face had vokert. He soon recovered himself. He sli7;:el</p>
        <p>"Tell me what</p>
        <p>his hanl under Merton's arm and thew him from the door. _</p>
        <p>"Come away, old chap. I'm afraid I've nothing pleasant to tell you." The trivial words jarred with the anguish in his eyes. John Merton broke away from him and hurried into the court. He opened the second door and disappeared into the house before David. could stop him.</p>
        <p>So great a change had passed over the face of John Merton by the time he left Gregory that it hardly resembled the face of the man who, an hour (Jr so before, had climbed these dark, rickety- st airs. His lips even were pale, under the strenuous• effort he made to calm himself, as he went into the- eitting-room of the house near the Edgware-road. He-found his father there alone.</p>
        <p>This was a fresh shock. John had hoped to find. his mother. He loved his father, and he had: always respected him; but the elder Merton was not clever, and his ideas were limited. John had always been in full sympathy with his mother. She . was not only clever, she had both tact and insight; she could always meet her son half-way. There could be no misunderstanding between them; while his father, with the best intentions, was often hopelessly dull about his son's wishes and ideas. John told himself that the slightest hint Nrould have put his loving, devoted mother in full cognisance of his misery; while with his father he must deal far more directly and painfully. After all, it developed itself more easily than ha had expected. "Father, I have just left the person named Gregory. I mean the man I have seen here with you, and also with mother." John waited, but no answer came. His father's- head dropped forward, his usually pale, withered cheeks reddened. -At last he said, wearily, with- out raising his eyes: "I knew it must come out one day. I told her so at the first, but she said, all must be right that was done for the sake of John."</p>
        <p>He raised his head and looked helplessly round him. - -</p>
        <p>1....... John put him into a chair. "Then this man's. tale is true ?" he said, sternly. "You have robbed him of his lawful inheritance so that I might. profit by it ? " For answer his father covered his face with his. hands. Presently he said: . "I always dreaded this. I told her You would not forgive it "—here he broke into a faint sob— "but she said you would never know." , John looked stilt taller and sterner as he Lowered over the crouching figure in the chair. , "Was there not honour and conscience? ' Was there not God to be reckoned with before you set all aside for me? How could you plan such a. robbery ? " 'John's heart-wrung tone struck his father like a blow. A terrible silence followed. Then tho father said, feebly: "It was not planned,- it—it came by degrees. The man's father would not employ his lawyer. He feared lest his wife should learn he had this 9bn. The father had gone up in the werld. He could not bear that any shadow should fall on his, name--" _</p>
        <p>"Did he invest this money in your name?" "Yes; in trust for this son. He was to have so much till he came of age, then he was to have the, whole. No one else knew. I was to manage everything. His valet, the other witness, died beore his master-did," _ .</p>
        <p>John walked up and down the room with despair in his heart. His father raised himself a little; he. had grown more composed. "Look here, John.. Before you say anything to• year poor mother I must tell you all the facts in excuse. The father died suddenly. The boy wag fifteen. He had been placed at a small private school, and he had already learnt to drink and gamble. Ile had a handsome face, and he preferred to associate with people beneath him. For a few years I paid him his allowance regularly. It was: not a large one, and he got into debt. They were disgraceful debts for a lad of his age; he was then nineteen.</p>
        <p>"You were just old enough to go to a public:- school. You were the best child parents ever had. We did not know how we could give you the education you deserved, and we grieved about this. Your mother grieved most. She said one day to- me Walter, why need you pay that young fellow's debts? Why not take that money as a, loan to be replaced, and send our darling to a. good school for a year? ' I did not at once con— sent. Little by little she persuaded me that if we. managed for a time without a servant she could soon save enough to -replace the loan. You went to school. Soon afterwards Gregory disappeared. I heard nothing of him for two years. When next we heard'of him your mother told me, it would never do to interfere with you. You had just taken• a scholarship which would have sent you to Oxford, only you were to joung.", John had checked his walk up and down; his father's flow of speech ixirprised him. He stood leaning in silent despair against the wall. Presently he spoke: "Father, oven then you could have told me; and I would have worked night and day to replace the money. Can't you see that you are answerable and that through you lam also answerable, for this fellow's.low vice and ,degraded character?" Old Merton looked sadly worried. "Your mother says that is not so. She says he would anyhow have been a drunkard, that .he must have a vicious nature—his schoolmaster said as much. He would never learn at schoob and he was always fond of drink. You sea we have always helped him. It is that which keeps us so poor. .He gets most of what you give your mother, John."' There was silence again. It was terrible to the son to feel that this crime had been committed for him; that this wretched Gregory had been allowed to go to ruin, so that he might attain. eminence.' Who could say what the poor fellow's future might have been under more trustworthy guardian- ship " The father presently spoke alain. • , "Of course, 1 know how you feel, John, and the way you take it shews me, I ought to have stood firm. You'll say what's the use of that now? Mind you, I'm perfectly sure that nothing would have saved Gregory from becoming a drunkard. He would have fooled away the money if he'd had it; and that's your mother's opinion. There's. one thing, my boy, I've got to ask you. Now I've seen the way you look at it, don't you say s wor&amp; to her, poor thing. She did it all for your sake. She took the sin, if it was one upon her soul for you, and you only. Be merciful and let her be, *John. I shall not give her a hint of your know— ledge. Think of what your mother has done for- both of us all her life. I can't "—his voice roses shrilly—" I won't have her made miserable." V.</p>
        <p>Weeks went by before Greer met his friend again. Then one day John Merton called on him. Greer felt embarrassed; but Merton took hiz: hand and shook it warmly. "I am come to say good-bye, old friend. I k you to send me now and then news of my father- 'and mother. I'm off to Canada; you shall have- s definite addreis as soon as I have one to send." Greer exclaimed and remonstrated; but opposi— tion was useless. His friend told him all was' settled. He was leaving England perhaps for ever, and he meant to settle in Canada. He was taking; with him poor drunken Gregory with the intention, if ho could, of reclaiming him. Again Greer protested. "Why," he asked "should everyone who loved John Merton be sacrificed to that sot? "</p>
        <p>John smiled sadly. "I feel leaving you, old chap "—ha wrung his friend's hand—"but, believe me, I am only -doing right. Gregory has been badly used ; it's time the poor fellow had a chance given him. So far if have had all the running."</p>
        <p>THE END.]</p>
      </div>
