      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Bad Trade and Out-Relief.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Bad Trade and Out-Relief.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, the Rev. 'l'. R. 'Matthews presiding. These were also present :—Mr. :Simpson, Mrs. Collingwood, Miss Broadbent, Rev. M. Ryan, Dr. Murray, Messrs. G. Mather, B. L. Bent, J. Billinge, W. Gill, It. Bolton, W. Furness,W. C. Brooks, A. Brooks, P. Longton, ' J. Cook, W. Flannagan, M. W. &amp;ton T. Ritchie, R. Forshaw, .1. Evans, A. Bottomley (clerk), R. W. Peacock (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery -(master) and A. Wright (superintendent of the Cottage Ilomes). .</p>
        <p>NUMBER Or INMATES. The MASTER reported that the number of inmates of the house was 514 as compared with 472 on the same date last year. CURIOUS QUESTION OF LIABILITY. The CLERK read a communication from the Local -Government Board forwarding, for the information of the Guardians, copy of a letter from the Town Clerk of Warrington with reference to the question of repay- ment by the Guardians of £3O 10s. paid by the Town Council in respect of the maintenance of a child named Mary E. Tompkinson in the Holy Trinity Certified Industrial School for Girls, Liverpool. In his letter the Town Clerk stated that on January 26th, Tomkinson who was 14 years of age and had been maintained in the pauper school of the Warrington Unionoras committed lor refractory conduct to the Holy Trinity Industrial School by the Warrington justices of the peace, upon the application of the Guardians of the Warrington 'Union. The child entered school on January 29th and !from that date until September 300, 1007, a weekly charge for maintenance of 7s. had been paid (on the .quarterly accounts being certified by the late Chief Constable) by the Watch Committee of the Borough. 'The total sum paid was £3O 10s. On January 7th, 1908, an account for maintenance for the quarter ending December 31st, 1907, was roceived by the Borough Treasurer and forwarded to the present Chief Constable Who discovered that the maintenance charges paid by the Watch Committee should have been borne by the Gun rdians and an account for £3O 10s. was rendered to them. The Clerk to the Guardians, whilst acknowledg- ing that they were morally responsible, could not on leg al greunds see his way to put the account forward for payment. The Local Government Board replied that it was not their practice to entertain applications for sanction nteept frara the autho r ity under whose account the Plyrnlnt, if male AvJuld be charged. They would, how- -ever, be prepared in the present case to entertain an application fiTru the Guardians for sanction to the PlYment of the sum in question so as to prevent any difflaulty at the audit. The CLERK said the Guardians were not bound to ,moat the payment, but if they thought fit to pay the s'3connt they could so with the consent of the Local 'Gore rument Board. It was decided that the Clerk should confer with the Town Cierk on the matter and report further. BAD TRADE AND OUT-RELIVE. .</p>
        <p>In accorda;;;lii-TnsTructions given at the last meeting, the Clerk tubmitted a statement showing the amounts expended in out-relief during the 16 weeks -elided July 18th, as compared with the tame period last :Year. The amounts were as follows :—Mr. Garnett. 8007. £5lB 10s. ; 1908, £693 16e. 2d. •, increase £175 51. ; Mr. Bowes, 1907, £584 153. 4d. ; 1908, £515 18s. 21. ; decrease £3B 17s. 2d. ; Mr. Auklund, 1907, £358 15s. ; 19A, £405 13s. 5d.; increase £47 18s. 3d. Mr. FURN ESS drew particular attention to the increase of £175 in Mr. Garnett's district and thought some explanation should tie given to the Board. Taking the whole of the Union,there was an increase for the sixteen weeks of £lBl ls. Gd. or /le average weekly incleaso of 11 10s, The Rev. M. RYAN (chairman of the Warrington Relief Committee), said the only explanation that could be given was bad trade. The people of Warrington knew very well how slack trade bad been of late, parti- "n•ularly in the iron works. It followed that tho only remedy for the people who suffered through being out .It.f work was to apply for relief. Mr. Garnett, who had had a long experience of the work, took just the same Vre now, if not more, as he did in times gone by. or the past week there was an increase in the applica- 'lnns from 85 in 1907 to 120. There was absolute destitution in a great many cases. The scale of relief hid down by the Local Government Board had not in • any case been exceeded and in many they had not reached the limit. The Committee were as careful as Possible. Mr. FURNFSS asked how it was that the relief in Mr. Garnett's district had increased so much more than in Mr. Aukland's when both were congested areas. Mr. Garnett showed an increase of £175 on an expenditure of £695, while in Mr. Aukland's case the advance was £l7 on £423. 'I he Rev. M, RYAN said the two districts were not in ihe same position. Mr. Glarnett'e district included Daliam and Bewsey Forges which since last Christmas 1 'had been in a very depressed state. Scarcely any of emplores of those works lived in Mr. Ankland's district. Then the factory, which effected Mr. Garnett's . district the most, had, been on short time and had also r: • 1,!,,41 a ten days' stoppage. Messrs. Monks, Hall and k-o.'s works were also in Mr.garnett's district and trade [ there had been depressed for some months. The fustian i cutting, industry, which would probably effect Mr. Ankland's district equally with Mr. Garnett's, was nlso very slack. The eorks which had been doing well— t• -Ole tanneries—were chiefly in Mr. Aukland's district. . Mr. FURNESS said Father Ryan's explanation was Perfectly satisfactory. .! Miss Brroannearr said that if they once got into an extravagant system there would be no end to it. She 1, It strongly that they ought to be guided largely by the advice of their relieving officers who knew the s, PenPle better than the auardians could. On one *nceasion she knew the committee. against Mr. Garnett's „ earnest advice, give relief in the Case of an able-bodied alt who refused to support his wife. The rapid Increase of out-relief was very serious. Mr. FORSFIANV said be was agreeably surprised that `e. the increase was not larger, considering the 'state of trade in Warrington. p Mr. GAnsurr was called into the room tux] con- tt firmed the Rev. M. Ryan's statement as to the causes •nr the increase. Mr. W. C. BROOKS; A certain amount of the money will be repaid? The CLERK replied in the affirmative and undertook tn Produce at the next meeting a return showing the elnonnt of repayments. The Rev. M. RYAN said that in his opinion the relief thsiness was rushed, As it was impossible to deal with 120 applications in an hour. Mr. W. C. BROOK:3 gave notice that at the next meet- ing he would move that the Board should meet at 11 -nistead of 10.39 in order to allow more time for relief ensiness. Mr. alaxnen said ho would move as an alternative that the relief committee begin its meetings at nine clock instead of 9.30. Mr. Foesnaw : Nine o'clock is too early for the applicants. Must of them would not be up then. .4Laughter.)</p>
        <p>BABY ACCIDENTALLY HANGED. TO keep her baby safely in bed a Fulham mother tied band round the little one. The child roust have 'oved or fallen, and the band tightened round its throat until it was strangled. A verdict of "Accidental 'death " was returned at the inquest on Thursday.</p>
        <p>The Going Away Season MPeople are running away to Sea- side and country in search of health. They feel run down, fag,ged out, easily tired, depressed. 0 3:i 0 fka, Food does not agree with them. 12...5a1: Appetite fails. The stomach revolts at the little that is eaten. 3:( Such people suffer while they are' away, and come back but little better. Change of air won't -cure an overworked digestion. 0 0 0 xMother Seigel's Syrcp. will. It is a natural remedy which will - make you feel better, at home or away, by toning up the whole digestive , system. Fresh air for the lungs and - Mother Seigel's Syrup for the stomach form the ideal combination.</p>
