ed went out in As I was returning three months before he that voiceless timer. I could leave India, he entrusted the stones to my e As he sat there, a firm hand pulled at the care, and, needless to say, I was followed by a bell with a clatter that sent a peal through the . Light of Rcouple of natives who should not be permanently who were determined that the Light to India. With the help of the captain of half-empty • house, and Clive started to his feet, for ho thought that a patient had' at last made l the ship. I evaded their attentions and en land- his way to Egis-street at the very time when the • lig fancied that .. for had or me given then; the.. slip; but they were too clever and bolero I was factotum had gone to her tea. But the (Men in the train at Dover I felt sure they were door revealed a telegraph boy, who thrust a yel- low envelope into his hand, and made off. Clive • after me. • So I thought it safer to. hide the looked aimlessly up and down the darkening : tones instead of bringing them to the i Castle, October street, where the early evening was and I placed them, as I remember now, n the sight, he went back to the consulting-room and i beginning to close in, and, seeing no one in ce-nouee own by the lake, where they are de- I posited under the third stone on the right-hand opened the telegram. ' side of the door. And it was well that I did so, He read it through, then staring stupidly at • for that night—" it read it again then looked up with a gasp. The address on the envelope was his own: Vereeer broke off suddenly, and Clive recog- "Dr. Clive Harley," nised the failing power of the wandering eyes y "15, Egis-street," and lips. " he said,- gently. Harley, the message. was addressed inside to Dr. C. "I can imagine what happened—the Indian en- "ComaLo teed our room and tried to ro Harley, London (try Egis-street), and it ran: r at once without delay; have wired you your room twenty pounds at Central Office; , case urgent; diamonds I Jasper Verplter nodded.. ".When he failed to seJoty.c,e Verelter, Rhondda Castle, Mertown, Dor. discover them, he said, with sudden energy, "he struck me. on the head, and must . have back against the table utterly overwhelme What did it mean? Clive Harley staggered stupefied me with some Indian drug, for I can d by remember nothing more —till t d I" o- ay the shock of the occurrence. He knew of no y one in the medical profession who bore the i He lay back utterly exhausted on the pil- same name as his own, and, besides that, thelows, and Clive Harle touched the electric bell message bore his address 'plain to read. The' at the side of the bed, and did not leave the pleasant thought struck him that this must room until both nurse , and doctor were este- have been despatched by, some person who had blished in charge, when he went back to Joyce\ read his letter to the `Scalpel" on "Brain I Vereker, for he was trembling very much, and Lesions," and been struck by it, or by some ,be felt that he must satisfy himself of the truth relative of a former patient of his in the Europa 'of that extraordinary story he had just heard Hospital; and the likelihood of the idea filled 1 before the night was much older. him with a vague sense of self-importance that • Joyce put on her hat and cloak and led the made him a new man. Carrie bad or good, he ' way into the dark, for they thought it better not Would obey the summons; and, pulling himself to trust anyone else with that strange story, and : together, he hastily packed a small bag and left ' when they unlocked the door of the ice-house, a message for the charwoman, then went in and let themselves in with pick-axe and lantern,' search of the funds waiting for him as thC it seemed to Clive at least as though he was office, and drew them without difficulty. I aotisting in a, midnight burglary. The weird pounds Ho had not had twenty in his pocket patches of shadow upon the dank, lichen-grown since he could remember, and the business-like t floor—the drop of the water from the roof mad. fashion in which the Clerk paid it over the desk • the girl shiver, as Clive lifted his pick. filled bins with a new and delicious sense of his .. Suppose—it should . not be 'there!" she own' importance: then, hastily throwing it few ! breathed: but the third blow of the iron lifted things together- in a hand-bag, lie hurried off to the stone, and there lay' the box of carved Waterloo, and caught the West-going express. 1 candid-wood wrapped in a handkerchief undis- . He was in the train before the whole wonder of turbed, as it had lain there for the last twenty recent events flashed over him in sequence. I years. By the light of the flickering lantent Suppose it tvas some whim of the operator at 'they looked at it together as it lay open in the telegraph office that had brought him lucre! i Chive's hands—with the marvellous diamonds Supposing that after all the message had been --inking at him from the jewellers' cotton. for ariother Dr. Harley, an not for him at all! .. d ri . . _ _ ' "This morning," said Clive Harley, breathing , e. .</p>
        <p>But as he sped along 'through the October night; , I quickly: "I wa's— too poor to buy bread to eat— be knew that he was ready to greet any enter- arid to-night I stand here heir to at least £60,C00 —and not ciuly heir to that, but heir to every- gency, since • his own affairs had reached a point of desperation: they could become no worse, thing—love--a career-and happiness—" and they might conceivably become much better! j His voiao was like a cry of triumph, and Joyce, looking. at him with a tremulous smile, felt her A smart motor-brougham was waiting for. him at the station, and although it was too dant to ' eyes sink under the look i 0 his that time might that was the of see the livery of the men on the front seat, yet germ same warmer feeling that the fur rug was soft and fine, and the lining of ' ripen to maturity in Congenial the carriage a delicate ivory cloth. He could , re as he sped along that the rod wound • through a gully with trees on either side, and that the general appearance of the landscape seemed ts be a vista of shadowy hills beyond. They drove in at a pair of great gates, and pre- sently Rhondda Castle loomed up before them, and they stopped under the swinging lamp over the entrance door that was of solid oak, clamped with iron, and in the centre of which a knocker. shaped like a grinning head, showed a light be- hind it. The door swung back at the sound of their arrival, and the white-haired butler reit down the steps. "Thank Cod! you are in time, sir!" he said, then, as Clive Harley stepped into the light, the man started back with a cry. "But you are not Dr. Harley!" he said: "you are not Dr. Harley 1 " "Indeed, I ern! " said Clive: "Miss Vero- ker's telegram reached me, and I came here ha- mediately please take me to her at once." But before the words were out of his lips, there came the swift rush of high-heeled shoes across the marble floor, and a girl moved swiftly forward.</p>
        <p>The sun hardly . penetrated further than the entry into Gaunt-street, that is one of the worst of the remaining London slums, and when young Clive Harley climbed up the dirty stairs of the rookery that was his father's address, and found himself at the door of an attic under the tiles,' his heart sank within him. The street, with the slatternly women at their doors, the filthy refuse in the gutter, made his heart sink, for what mad- ness cculd have induced his father to. spend the declining days of his life in such a spot, was diffi- cult for him to imagine. When he opened the door, the clean bareness of the whitewashed attic struck him with a sense of chill. There was no Turniture in it, save a bed, a rug, and a chair,• but from the wall the picture Of his dead mother smiled down at him! with her sweet eyes. On his knees, by the embers of a small charcoal fire, ! knelt a thin elderly man who was cooking some- thing in a small pan, and he lifted his head up las the door opened: "Conic in!" he said, Whoever you are—and shut the door behind !pm, for I am--" But when he saw Clive, tha irritation of his voice broke in a cry; "Clive!" I he said, "Clive I"</p>
        <p>"Is that Dr. Harlez, Anderson?" she said, ii a clear, high-bred VOICie, and Clive hastened up to meet her.</p>
        <p>Dim memories of his father's face surged bsick on the young man from the far away past. %.id he stared at the gaunt, wasted features now trac- ing the faint blurred likeness to what he could remember. "Father!" he said, "What are you \ doing here in this wretched place?" Then, as the sordid poverty of his surroundings orougnt rue trutn to aim with a nasn, ms mous heart cried out: ",Oh father—you have been starving yourself for me—so that I should never know it!"</p>
        <p>"But you—are not Dr. Harley I" she cried, in sudden dismay, Its her hand went out: "I thought there could be only one Clive Harley in London--though if my father ever told me the</p>
        <p>address, I hare fotgotten it." The distress upon her face was so poignant TTEtrlav hesitated. " I will do whufever you think best," ne saw : " if, as I gather, it is your father who is ill, I may be able to relieve him 1 "-</p>
        <p>Clive llarley the elder stood like a e‘eivizted</p>
        <p>criminal before his son. 'Don't blame me, Clive," he said, hoarsely. "I invested nearly all my savings in diamonds hi India—and they were lost. Then I sent all the remnant of my fortune to you, for I knew that there was not enough for both of us, and yours was the young life with the future before it—and I should only have been a drag upon you—l kept back what I calculated would allow me ten shillings a-week, till I died—but one cannot guard against illness, and my, money clipped away too quickly; but I could not leave London, lad, for sometimes—l could see your face without yoUr knowledge. Eut it will have to be the' workhouse now, for there is not another shot left in the locker."</p>
        <p>He ended with an attempt at a laugh that turned to a Eel', and Clive caught his chill hand and gripped it. "Father!" he cried, "Look up, father—all your wretched days are over—for the Rajah's diamonds are found, and I have brought them to you, so that you are a rich man now!" And he laid the sandal-wood box in his father's arms.</p>
        <p>t Fast, present, and future met in the little house in Egis-street that night, whither Clive had brought his father. And so out of the dark- cess of despair there dawned a glorious morn- ing, made brighter by the light that shone from I the unselfishness of a father's devolion. [TI-a. END]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>YOUR SKIN IN SUMMER. How to Avoid Danger and Discomfort.</head>
        <p>YOUR SKIN IN SUMMER. How to Avoid Danger and Discomfort.</p>
        <p>• To aveid the dangers and discomforts arising from • sun freckles, spots, peeling noses, and other disorders which result from excessive perspiration or dryness in summer, regular anointings with Zam-Buk— the ;balm-that jztet suits the mutton and the skid —are • necessary. Handq, faces, and necks are, of course,the parts most liable to suffer from the heat rays ; but in the armpits and other joints serious sores may arise from chafing and excessive perspiration, while on the chest nod back rashes and eruptions are prevalent and discomforting. I Zam-Buk, which is prepared only from pure heibal essences, is delightfully cooling and soothing, and the balm is so refined that it soaks through the skin and I restores to it that much wished for softness, clearness, and velvety " feel of health. Don't fail to take a I box of Zam-Buk with you en your holiday, also a box ! of Zam-Buk Medicinal Soap. Remember the scratching of a " heat lump "may result in eczema or ulceration i,hut if you use the Zam-Buk balm, all danger will be I removed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>MX LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>MX LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Winwick Free Church.</p>
        <p>Special services were held in the above church on Sunday afternoon ond evening. The service in the afternoon was taken by the Rev. W. Mellor (Wesleyan), of Padgate, who gave an address on "The Compassion of our Saviour," and in the evening the Rev. H. Wilson (ceneregationalist), of Ashton-in- Makerfield, bp( ke en " The Resurrection." Consider- able interest was added to the services by the rendering of anthems by the choir, and hymns by the children attending the newly-formed Sunday school and Band of Hope. The anthem "The Lord is my shepherd" was rendered in the afternoon and in the evening "The sun shall be no more" and "As pants the bard " the solos being taken by Mr. E. Smethurst, of Bolton. The weather beinp fine the attendance at both services was far above the average, and the collections amounted to nearly £4, which sum will be devoted to the building fund. Miss F. E Ormiston, who presided at the organ, had trained the children. This Free Church, which was the second to be built in the country under the auspices of the Notional Free Church Union, has a membership of nearly 30 members; services are held weekly and the sermons are preached voluntarily by members of the various Nonconformist Churches in the district, both by ministers and laymen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Padget° Wesleyan Chard.</head>
        <p>Padget° Wesleyan Chard.</p>
        <p>• The Rev. E. Lloyd-Jones of Manchester, and formerly of this circuit, preached two effective sermons on Sunday last in the above church. On Monday evening he gave a lecture entitled " Pilgrim's Progress." In the course of his address he said 11 at John Dunsan was born in the year IE2B, and died in 1688. God's method of giving great men was to give them in a cluster—all at once. There was not an even distribution of them. They only came periodically and if they (the audience) would look, they would find that in one period they had great men in abundance, whilst the previous period had not a single specimen of greatness. If anyone naked him what he considered the leading characteristic of the age, he would say that it was the absence of great men. lie did not know a single man or woman of the present day, but were quite second clasp either in philosophy, science or theology. Let them (he congregation) look on the life of the political world of that time, who had they: Penn, Hampden, Ray, Hyden, and plenty without Cromwell, who had brains enough to stock seven kings and plenty to spare. John Bunyan lived in the age of great men; but he was absolutely independent of them. He (Mr. Jones) knew his " Progress " very well, and be knew the literature of that period well, but be had failed to recognise one paragraph, one thought, one illuetration that Bunyan either Legged or borrowed from any of his contempories. What was the secret of that little book ? It's theory of religious experience was so true to Christians, so true to fact, so true—a strange word to use there—to psyckology, or the science of the human mind. There were thousands who hail teen captured by its illustutions of religions experience. They saw, that he (Mr. Jones) said illus- trations. Runyan was too broad minded, too observant to run nll religious experience into one word. The supreme illustration was the one noted in the character of Christian. They would ask what sort of a religious experience he bad, that he the lecturer) made it a sample? Religious life started in deep painful despair- ing penitence. Strange to say a lecture written by James on religious experience declared that on scientific grounds, the religious experience that supported any deep and painful penitence was the most rational, most adequate and the most prominent. The rev, gentleman thought Buyan was a Baptist ; but they would never find that he was by reading "Pilgrim's Progress." He was a Baptist in much the same sense as Dr. MacLaren and Dr. Clifford were.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Ellesmere. Street Wesleyan Mission.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere. Street Wesleyan Mission.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary services in connection with the above place of worship were held on Sunday. The morning service was held in the Mission .Hall, the preacher being the Rev. W. Hollingsworth, S. T. L. Quebec (formerly lay preacher in the Warrington Circuit). There was a very fair congregation and the preacher delivered an eloquent sermon upon the teat • For ye shall not go but with haste, nor go by Eight ; for the Lord will go before you; and the o'o of Israel will he your reward" (Isaiah Hi. 12). The anthem "Onward Christian Soldiers" was sung by the choir. In the afternoon a musical service was held in Bold Street Wesleyan Church. Mr. J. eri. Withinshaw presided, and a helpful address was given by Mrs. James Dodds. Mr. L. Richardson ably rendered the solo "Waft her engeis," and Miss A. Beswick and Mr. J. flannan were heard to advantage in the duet " Children, pray this love to cherish." The evening service was also held in Bold-street Church, and the pulpit was again occupied by Mr. Hollingsworth, who preached a helpful sermon from the words, " And as Jesus passed by. he saw a man named Matthew, sitting at the receipt of custom." The duet" Watchman, what of the night " was impressively rendered by Messrs. L. Richardson -and W. Clay, and the anthem "Abide with me," was given by Bold-street choir. On Monday evening a social evening was held, presided over by the Rev. T. Miller, Mr. J. Ellison in a few n ell-chosen words presented to .the Rev. W. Hollingsworth, on behalf of the teachers of the school, a handsomely bound copy of "Elijah." Mr. flollingsworth suitably responded, and later he delivered an interesting lecture on "Romance and reality in Newfoundland." Refresh- , ments were handed round during an interval. A pleassnt and anccessful anniversary was brought to a close with the singing of" God be rvith you till we meet again."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TAKE MIS TO-DAY TO YOUR CFIENITST FOR THE NEW REATEITY FOR NERVES, STOMACH AND KIDNEYS. COSTS ONLY A FEW PENCE.</head>
        <p>TAKE MIS TO-DAY TO YOUR CFIENITST FOR THE NEW REATEITY FOR NERVES, STOMACH AND KIDNEYS. COSTS ONLY A FEW PENCE.</p>
        <p>A new remedy has lateay been brought to light which is now being recommended and prescribed everywhere. Tt is made from a famous prescription by a noted specialist, and is called Dr. Cassell's Tablets. It costs only a few pence, and we advise all persons, young or old, who are suffering from any form of nerve or bodily weakness, or such complaints as indigestion, weakness of the kidneys and back, palpitation, loss of flesh or appetite, weak lungs, and those who rve in any way thin, weak, nervous, or 'badly developed, to try these tablets. Stout people may take them without fear of increase of adipcse tissue, because of their extraordinary power of converting fat into sound healthy flesh, blood, bone and;rnuscle. The price is only 10),d., larger sizes Is. lid. and 2s. 9d., and any chemist will supply Dr. Cassell's Tablets. The public aro to be congratulated in, now being ablo to seems this famous remedy, for everyone is astonished at its Marvellous strengthening effect.</p>
