      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING AT APPLETON HALL.</p>
        <p>The Fear of Invasion.</p>
        <p>In the picturesque grounds of Appleton Hall, the residence of the President (Mr. T. IL Lyon) the members of the Warrington branch of ithe National Service League held their fifth annnal general meeting on Saturday 'afternoon. Fortunately the weather was :deal and a large number took the opportunity of spending a few hours amidst such cot-menial surroundings. The meet- ing wits held in a marquee and was presided over by Colonel:the lion.M .Tal hot. Amongst others present were: —Admiral Penrose Fitzgerald,Mrs.Roland Trimen,Lady Stamford, Mrs. Ussber, Sir Forbes and Lady Adam, the sMayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst) and the Misses Smethurst; Mies Macdougal, Major Molyneux Seale end Miss Seale, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Roberts, Mrs. Broadbent and the Misses Broadbent, Mr. and Mrs. Hutchings, Miss Massy, Major and Mrs. Marshall, Mr. and Mrs. Vete B. Davies Mrs. Edelsten, Captain Howard Vise, Major and Aft% Edridge, Miss C. Brook, Rev. and Hon. C. F. Cross, Rev. H. V. Pigot, Mrs. Pigot and the Misses Pigot, Miss C. Brook, Mr. and Mrs. .1. J. Crosfleld, Mrs. Stack, Miss Crosfield, Major Strachan (organizing secretary for Lancashire), and Captain G. R:Crostield (hon. secretary of thelVarrington branch and also for Lancashire.) Captain CROSFIELD said tbeir President, Mr. Lyon, ‘eas unfortunately forbidden b 3 his medical adviser to preside and they must all heartily regret his absence. Colonel Talbot. a very distinguished soldier who left the army for the sole purpose of pushing the National Service League -and was devoting his life to going about the country and stirring the people up, had kindly consented to tale the chair. (Applause). The CHAIRMAN said numerous letters of regret had been received. Mr. A. 11. Crosfleld, M.P. telegraphed to Mrs. Lyon as follows:—"My wife and I so much regret that being in London we, cannot avail ourselves of your very kind invitation to attend to-day's meeting. We trust you will have more settled weather than we have here to-day." They were glad that Mr. Crostleld's hope had been realized. (Applause.) Continuing, Colonel Talbot said that during the last three years in which be had been working actively for the League, perhaps the commonest objection that had been brought against their proposals was that owing to our insular position we bad to trust to the navy and to the navy alone, and that anything further was unneces- sary. Ho yielded to no-one in his admiration for what the Royal Navy bad done for the country in the past nor in confidence as to what it would do in the future' but it was evident to everyone who thought about the' question that the sphere of action of e. navy was necersarily limited. As the Sultan of Tuley remarked on one occasion "Iron-clads cannot climb bills." iLaughter.) It was a matter of peculiar satisfaction that they had with them that afternoon a very distin- guished officer of the Royal Navy, Admiral Penrose Fitzgerald, and that they would also have an address from Mrs. Roland Trimen, who was indefatigable in promoting their cause, especially in the Richmond and Oxford district. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>The annual report stated that the branch now con- sisted of 49 tr embers and 1,122 associates, a considerable increase over last year. The work of educating public opinion ia Warringtonss to the necessity, justice and advantage of universal military training bad been quietly and systematicaly carried forward. Two very successful public debates had been keld, at which the two following resolutions : "That universal training as proposed by the N.S.L. tends towards the pro- motion of peace " and "That Lord Roberts' proposals are necessary and desirable," were carried by e large majority in both cases. In addition to those debates several other meetings had been held an various parts of the town, organized by the branch honorary helpers. It was impossible to speak too highly of the work done by those honorary helpers, thanks to whom members were kept in personal touch, journals were distributed free of cost, shooting encouraged and superintended. Great interest in shooting had been shown, the number of those firing on the afternoons set apart for the League by the Appleton Rifle Club numbering anything between 50 and 100 on many afternoons. Two excellent matches took place between Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons and Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., each firm winning one match. The ennqnerer wns to he fired off this year. Considering the Lumber of well.meaning enthusiasts who seemed to think that all talk of rreparation for war smacked ot the dodo; con- sidering the distractions of robbery and confiscation, votes for women, one man one pension, and a dozen other political cries, the progress of the idea of universal military training had been distinctly encouraging. The advance, however, beforo they won over the enemy was still long and arduous. "Ready aye ready" must be their motto. When they were indeed ready it would be time to rely on peace. Not indeed a readiness which waited till the fighting had begun before serious training WRS undertaken, but a preparation "soberly and by custom taken." Did they condemn Mr. Haldane's Territorial Scheme? By no means, Every tine leaguer must feel that he owed a debt of gratitude t 3 Mr. Haldane for having given him the framework for a real national army. But because they backed up Mr. Haldane's scheme so far as lay in their power, on the principle that half a loaf was better than no bread at all, it must not prevent them from stating clearly, emphatically, authoritatively that the serious training must be given before war broke out and not afterwards; that it was criminal to expect men who had bad but a few days training in camp to fight against thoroughly disciplinedltroops who had been trained for two years or more. Their duty was clear: to work through voluntary towards national service, even as their fore- fathers worked through voluntary towards national ducation. 1 ADMIRAL FITZGERALD ON GERMAN INTENTIONS.</p>
        <p>Admiral PlitißoSE FITZGERALD, in reeving the adoption of the report, congratulated tho Warrington branch on the very large number -of workingmen associates which pointed to the excellent spade work that must have been done by the members of the League. The hon. secretary told him that the main credit was due to the honorary helpers. Continuing the Admiral said they must consider the naval and military situation nhich had arisen in consequince of the very rapid rise of the German navy and the appear- ance of Germany as already the second naval power in Europe, if not in the world. The situation, he con- tended, demanded without delay a national army founded on the only secure and stable basis, that of national service, in order to set free our navy to per- form the very essential duty of safeguarding our food supplies. Five-sixths of the food of this country came across the sea and we would starve in six weeks if it were not protected. We had been challenged both in word and .deed and it was no use closing our eves to the fact. "The twentieth century belongs to 'Germany. Germany's future is on the ocean. Germany must have such a navy that the strongest navy in the world will hesitate to try conclusions with it.' Those were the words and the deeds were the feverish haste with which Germany was building iron clads of the most powerful type, with very small coal capacity, intended to he used only in the North Sea. The speaker referred to the fact that we had had to concentrate nearly the whole of our battleships in the North Sea. He would be the last person to belittle the navy or to suppose that it would not act up to its reputation when the day of battle came, but if we were going to tie the navy to the North Sea Itecauce we had not got an army fit to resist invasion, it would not be able to do its proper duty in protecting our food supplies and our enormous commercial interests all over the world. The situation already should awaken everybody to be anxious about it. We bed bad to withdraw nearly all our ships from foreign stations because the men were wanted to man the iron dads in the North Sea and we heard from Consuls and merchants all over the world of the injury to British trade and prestige because the Brit,ish flag was hardly ever seen in China, the Pacific, the East or West Indies. We did not dare , now to send the bulk of our battleships four days sail from the East Coast of England. It was said that other rations could not attack us without cause and warning. n'hat utter rubbish ! Someone had calculated that five- sixths of the wars which had broken out had done so I without any declaration of war at all. What were we doing to meet this menace ? Theta was the Territorial Army scheme and he wished to speak with the greatest respect of It. He believed that Mr. Haldane was work- ing absolutely on the right lines. He was erecting the frame work of a national army hut unfortunately he was not filling it up. The speaker criticised the scheme in some detail and contended that the defence of our land should not be left to the whim of young men. We , should make RI% national duty and then it would be no ' hardship, as all would be treated alike. Under the new scheme so many excuses for non-attendance at drill would have to be allowed that it seemed exceedingly doubtful whether the new Territorials wonld have as much drill :as the old Volunteers had, and we bad, it on the authority of our best soldiers, including Lord Roberts, that the Volunteers, patriotic and energetic as they were, were not sufficiently drilled to have any chance against the highly trained and organized troops they would have to meet in case the country were invaded. It should be a point of honour with Englishmen that they would take such measures to provide for the 'safety of the country that under God's good providence they might be able to hand down to their children the great inheritance they bad received. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>GOVERNIIIENT AND EMPIRE DAY.</head>
        <p>THE GOVERNIIIENT AND EMPIRE DAY.</p>
        <p>Mrs. ROLAND TRIMIN. in seconding,said the Govern- ment of the day appeared to regard our nktional flag with such extreme veneration and respect that they bed absolutely refused to expose it to the corumon public gaze, even on a high day and holiday like Empire Day. That was a most deplorable refusal, because it not only deeply insulted and grieved our great self-governing colonies but it put a deadly moral and weapon into the hands of our rivals and enemies for they heldon common with a great many of ourselves, that when a nation cr an individual had learnt to be-little</p>
        <p>or think lightly of the symbol of the country's honour and greatness, to look upon that flag as a piece of the common bunting, that nation or individual was on the high road to losing that honour and greatness, because what was held lightly was lost lightly. Mrs. Trirnen then proceeded to criticise the late Government. Although, she said, they did not decrease the army like Mr. Hairline had dons to the tune of 37,509 men, although they did not weaken our Navy as Lord Tweedinouth did, still they were guilty of criminal neglect of their country's safety and welfare when they refused to consider the strong recommenda- tions of the Norfolk Commission which consisted of first class fighting men like Lord Roberts, Sir Evelyn Wood and others who reported that the state of affairs—it was much worse now—was a grave national danger and that in their opinion the only way to remedy it was to introduce as soon as possible legislation for universal compulsory military training. The Unionist Government, led by Mr. Balfour, backed by a strong majority, shelved that report and did net even give it a decent funeral. Not only that, but they treated those i I lustr ions, patriotic experts, who had no axes to grind, with contempt and obloquy. They had dared not only to diagnose the disease bitt to say what the remedy was, and that remedy did not happen to be convenient to the party politicians—those curses of our country—and neither the Government nor the opposition lifted a finger in the matter. Nor was it convenient to the party politicians of the present day. It was perfectly ridiculous in a democratic country to expect the Government, however well intentioned and however strong it might be, to pass a measure which had not the great majority of the electorate behind it. The respon- sibility therefore rested with the electorate and particu- larly with the workingmen who formed the majority. Great Generals told us that invasion was not onlY possible but probable. The great German military authorities told us it was not only possible and probable but that the invasion would take place at the very moment when the German Government, which was the most astute and ambitions of European Govern- ments, considered that the hour had come for taking that great step forward, for striking that blow for the advancement of their world empire. Mrs. Trimen then urged the responsiblity of defending our empire, and said that if we could not help the colonieti when they needed our aid, we should be bankrupt in gratitude and honour. She asked the women to acquaint themselves with ambulance work so tos to be able to tend and succour the men who braved the dangers of the battle- field for their sakes. Real patriotism did not consist only of waving flags and singing songs. It meant that they must love their country better than themselves and their families. Let them urge upon the Government that this question of universal training must have pre- cedence over any other, and place themselves in the path of duty which was the path of safety. (Applause.) The report was unanimously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>tALL RIGHTS RESERVXD.]</head>
        <p>tALL RIGHTS RESERVXD.]</p>
        <p>THE HEELS OF CHANCE.</p>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>PROGRESS OF THE WORK IN LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Major STR AC ILAN moved :—That the Executive Corn- mittee for the following year consist of Mr. T. 11. Lyon. President; Dr. K. IL Markel, Vice-President; Miss S. Broadbent, Rev. W. Bracecamp, Mrs. Edelsten, Mr. H. Field, Mr. E. .1. Hall, Lady Lilford, Mrs. Lyon, Mr. H. Owen, Mr. H. Roberts, Mr, J. If. Smethurst and Captain G. R. Crosfield (hon. see.) ; and that the General Committee consist of the above together with Mr. A. Beck, Mrs. D. Fairclough, Mrs. B. Fairclough, Mr. C. Haslam. Mr. F. W. Monks, Air. W. P. Rylands, 1 Dr. R. Smith. Rev. Canon Willis, Rev. Father Wilson. He said the league had now eight branches in Lanca- shire and a county committee had been formed consist- ing of about fifty of the leading residents. They were delighted that the present Lord Derby, had succeeded his father as president. (Applause.) From his experi- ence in Lancashire he (the speaker) was convinced that if the Government would bring about. the adoption of the scheme the League was advocating it would be accepted by the people with far less demur than compulsory education was in 1870. Miss Cnosrrchn in seconding said they had first to convert public opinion and the two sreat obstacles were prejudice and artnthy.Honeet prejudice they most respect. People had a perfect right to their own points of view and there was a great deal of genuine prejudice against the League's proposals. There certainly Nes in Wales and she supposed there was in Warringtort* Sbe was talking to an honourable and useful citizen of Waits the other day and as soon as she mentioned the Empire he said "Empire ! Our Empire was founded by robbers, it is upheld by tyranny and cemented by blood." It was cemented by blood, but some of that blood was the best and bravest we had in the country. She said " You cannot say that the Volunteers are otherwise than most peaceful and law abiding citizens" but he replied" I am sorry to say I have watched the Volun- teer movement from its beginning and it has done nothing but make for.bloodshodd and aggression ever since it was started."- When they knocked against reasoning like that it WAS hopeless. (Laughter.) 1 here vras nothing to be said in favour of apathy and it behoved all members of the League to do their utmost to dispel it. The resolution was unanimously agreed to. VOTE OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. ROBERTS moved a vote of thanks to the chairman and speakers and to Mr. and Mrs. Lyon. He observed that he was ona of those good Liberals, of whom he fancied there were not quite a majority present that afternoon but still a sufficiently largo number to justify him in making an appeal to them on the lines that Mrs. Trimen so ably and eloquently put. He claimed that the proposals of the National Service League should commend themselves to the best and truest Conservatives and to the most democratic Liberals. He could not pretend to explain what Conservatism ices. He had hb; own ideas about it. (Laughter). He would leave it to the Conservatives present to accept it from Mrs. Trimen that the pro- posals of the League were consistent with the best and truest Conservatism and be would tale upon himself to say, as a good Liberal and a life-long Liberal, that the democratic principle which was involved in the principles of the League which made no differ- ence between Duke's son and cook's son,which required the sons of every man to take their part in being trained for the defence of the country, was democratic in the truest and best sense. Therefore he appealed with confidence to Liberals to get rid of the prejudice begotten of the Liberal training of twenty or thirty years ago, to consider the enormous development which had taken place in the longitude and latitude of the boundary of the empire since then, and to remember that the politics which did for twenty or thirty years ago in regard to this great question would not do for to-day. We bad entered upon a great and magnificent heritage and we should be worse than traitors if we failed to do our duty in preserving it for those who came after us. He hoped with all his heart —in fact he had no doubt about it—that in a very few vears time, the question would be taken up not by the Liberal Party or the Conservative party but by a great national party which would put other and minor things aside and take up this question and see to its full accomplishment. ( Applause.) The MAYOR in seconding said that when he read that the Government would not display the flag on Empire Day he felt it was a disgrace to England. He was, however, happy to say that the day was well observed in Warrington. (Applause.) The vole WAR heartily accorded and the CHAIRMAN briefly responded. During the meeting the Apollo Male Voice Choir led the singing of'" Land of hope and glory," (Elm), " A song of service" (Florence Aylward) and "England is calling" (Truths Crostield). After the meeting tea was served, and the visitors were then at liberty to inspect the well kept gardens. The hand of the 4th Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment was in attendance and played choice selections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>DAILY TRIUMPHS.</head>
        <p>DAILY TRIUMPHS.</p>
        <p>Day by day confidence increases. Success merits reward. Warrington people know-a" good thing." They praise it when they find it. Mr. Thomas Rigby, 121, Church-street, \V.lrrim74 says :—" lam grateful for the benefit I have lecrived from Dean's pills, and I have no objectipn to the facts of my case being published. " For between five and six years I was a great sufferer from kidney complaint, the worst part of the ailment being severe pains in my back, which was often so bad that I could not get about. I was almost afraid to stoop, because it was so cliflicult to raise myself. "I took medicine for three years, hut it did me no good at all. It was different, however, when I began with Doint's pills, for they helped me from the first end gradually brought me back to health. (Signed) Thomas Rigby." No one can be healthy whose kidneys are not healthy. The kidneys filter the impurities and Poisons from the blood. The health of the whole body depends on them. Diseased or weak kidneys are the cause of many most dangerous diseases, such as Dropsy, Gravel, Stone, Urinary Troubles, Rheumatism, Lumbago and Sciatica. These ills are warnings of even more serious trouble to come if they are neglected, but are relieved and cured by Dean's Pills. Bonn's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists aad stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. You are sure to get the right medicine if you Ask distinctly for DOAN'S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>scornsll NATIONAL EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>scornsll NATIONAL EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>The trades holiday week of Edinburgh,and Glasgow has naturally drawn large crowds out to Sanghton Park, and special attractions have been arranged. Some splendid tennis courts have been prepared, and a big tournament is now in progress till August Ist. The programme includes both open and invitation events. Wingate's Prize Band the winner of many trophies, including the thousand guinea one at the Crystal Palace is meeting with a great reception. This combination is supplemented by the band of the Queen's Own Oxfordshire Hussars. For the week commencing July 27th, the management have succeeded in engaging the famous Belgian Band of the Peter Benoit Musicale Societe, Antwerp, composed of fifty picked musicians who annually have to compete for their position in the band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
