      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>JULY 25th, 1908.</p>
        <p>The demeanour of the THE Opposition towards the CONSPIRACY Licensing Bill is, as we OF TALK, anticipated would be the case, particularly wortlk Watching. It is obstruction striking a new atti- tude under revised conditions. " Hon, gentlemen opposite " are determined that, so far as they can control the situation, the Bill shall not be dis- cussed in its vital parts. The time allotted by the Panru 3lrgizmr. with a not ungenerous hand,</p>
        <p>is to be wasted as far as possible over merely dilatory amendments, as to how many of which' -even the Opposition itself is not agreed For example, Mr. GREETTOD, who is a member of the firm of Bass and Co., opened out proceedings on the Bill by moving an amendment, the purpose of which was to exempt places with a population of 10,000 cr under from the reduction of licences Which might possibly take place under Clause I. tie was anxious that the villager should not be shut out from what is his only club. If 'the scale of reduction proposed by the Dill is carried out, he observed, it would inflict great hardship on the humble people of the rural districts, and disorganise their whole scheme of life—a pathetic picture of what is not iU the least likely to occur. EMI Mr. WALTER Loxo, who has now become a sort of deputy .8 tder, hoped that the ameadineut would not be pressed. It would, he said, set up an invidious distinction between urban and rural districts (as it obviously would), and the amendment, after it had occupied an amount of time that might have been usefully spent, was at length withdrawn. The B:11 is being debated by the Opposition on the wholly unjustifiable assumption that it pro_ vides for the suppression of licenses on a wholesale 'and absolutely non-intelligent plan. Mr. ASQUITH awakened the ire of 31r. AUSTEA CHA.URF.ELAIN 00 Monday by interjecting a remark to the effect that it would be advisable to read the Bill before offering criticisms upon it. Mr. llannanr S.tamt exPlained this observation by saying that if 11°Q. members opposite had taken the trouble t° read tha measure which is under dis- cussion, together with its scheda!es, they would refrain from making soma of their objec li°os. For our own part we are far frun being sure that this would be the cis% Tie oPponents of the Government may quite possibly have a competent understanding of the Bill and yet speak as if they were ignorant of its provisions. What they desire is that the measure shall be rnis-</p>
        <p>understood in all the constituencies. That is part Of the game they are playing, which possibly seems to be more astute than it really is. Mr. GRRTION t'llked, for instance, as if all the old roadside inns are to be closed, but„ he cannot possibly be ignorant of the social nature of the Government Proposals as seriously to suppose that they contain Coy proposition so sweeping and so certain to be almost universally distasteful. It is not a Prohibition Bill that is being dealt with by the House of Commons, but a Bill that do no more than reduce the number of licences 'Which is almost everywhere in excess of public ileeds. It is saddening to think that it should be Lecessary to utter such a truism as this; and yet It is really very necessary to say it, not only once, but many times over. The trickiness of the °PPosition is not being exposed ip the House of Commons so thoroughly as it might be, for .entirely good reasons. It is essential that the "Porters of the Government shout I refrain frern playing into the hands of the Opposition by taeßus of frequent speeches that would be 'erluivafent to taking part in a conspiracy to pre vett the consideration of the Bill throughout and t° weaken its position when it goes up to the liouse of Lord 4. The self-reitraint of Liberal luembers in this respect is admirable. So also is that of members of the Givernment, some of Whom are compelled by the necessities of their D3sitioo to rise fregliontly to their feet, but who e°tldense what they have to say into the fewest ilosaible number ef sentences. There is goo I 7*eason, the7efore,to think that the conspiracy will defeat itself, and that the main points of the Bill Will be voted upon in the ordinary course—an Object which frequent speech-making from the Liberal benches would most certainly defeat. Warrington was doubly POSTMASTER AND honoured on Thursday POST OFFICE, when it became possessed of a new and handsome K ost office and received a :isit from a member of the Ministry in the rerscn of the Right Hon. 3'dney Bu,,ton, Postmaster-General, who per- ()ruled the task of declaring the building oren -After this brief ceiernony there was a more 1114,Posinc, function in the Parr Hall, when the 7,-(eruber"for the Borough entertained Mr. Buxton "4d a representative gathering of Warrington's 'Public men to luncheon. Interesting speeches wade by the host and the principal pest, Mr. Crodield taking the opportunity till rut in another word on behalf of Wages of local postmen as affected by Mr. .„.uxton's new classification scheme. The Borough Vraber has already earned the gratitude of the Coar.rington Postmen's Federation, and they are png they will be able shortly to thank the wttreaster General also for his consideration. the believe there is good ground for the hope, c;rgh Mr. Buxton touched upon almost all to.rent post office topics but this in replying to the St Of his health. His speech was calculated to tirest the audience he was addressing, and very 5e attention was paid to his illuminating 031)0titi0n of recent and prospective post et) reforms. The topic does Dot appeal</p>
        <p>_,</p>
        <p>-77,77-- 7 -</p>
        <p>naturally to one, but Mr. Buxton inter- spersod his remarks with so maey flishes of ready wit that the speech must be ranked as one of the most entertaining delivered under the roof of Parr Hall. It covered all the ground between the influence of cheap postage in cementing the good p?rsonal relations between the people of different nations and the villainy of the small boy who damages so many of the Post.naster's insulators with stone-throwing. Terminated as they wore by the advent of the suffragette; the day's pro- ceedings wore of such implrt and interest as to ensure their being long memorable.</p>
        <p>Warrington is got ting AD, EraPING advertised—free! We have WAIIRINGTON. done a little of the adver-</p>
        <p>tising, and now the " Clarion " has takon a hand. Bat the organ of the Socialists doss not favour the idea of War- rington advertising itself as an industrial centre. After referring rudely to Warrington as " a congeries of dingy brick-and-.nortar tenements," the " Clarion " says with delicf.ta Graysonian sarcasm, " Why endeavour to improve it ? Why not, indeed, make it bigger, smokier, dirtier, and slummier ? " We might answer, " Because the criminal folly of doing so is appa-cent even to commercially-minded Warringtonians." It will be remembered that at the last Council meeting, Alderman Bennett protested against "the one idea of bigness . . . a huge Widnes or a bigger St. Helens," and for this the "Clarion clasps him to its heart. We can hardly imagine the embrace leaving the roost delectable of flavours, for the Alderman is not so. Socialistic as he sometimes sound; and looks. The "Clarion " and he might soon get across if anyone mentioned Tariff Reform ! Our Socialist friends are welcome to their bit of fun, but they should recollect that Socialism does not make bread and butter, whereas manufactories do. If they and the town are properly managed—end with so much enlightenment at home and elsewhere they ought to be—there is no reasoa why Warrington should not grow bigger and better at the same time. Port Sunlight, Bournville, and Letch- worth prove that industry and manufacture may be fostered, and the externals of existence brightened and improved, at one and the same time. Even the "Clarion" works might be trans- ferred to Warrington without our becoming much dirtier and sluminier." So let us advertise ! advertise !</p>
        <p>The House of Lords read Ter. PEERS AND the O'd Age Pensions Bill PENSIONS, for a second time on Toes- day, because they dare not do anything else. Lord Lansdowne admitted as much. The Bill is not, as he said on another occasion, "favourable ground" for a dispute between the Commons and the Lords. But if the Lords dare not refuse the second reading of the Bill they succeeded in expressing their dislike to it pretty clearly. An assembly of the wealthiest men in the country is asked to consider a proposal that the poorest should, on reaching the age of seventy, receive a pension of 5L a week from the State. One after another they rise to tell us that this is the beginning of ruin—ruin to the country, ruin to the Empire, ruin to the moials and character of the poor. The State, they tell us, cannot aft ,rd to find the money, and, if it is found, the poor will be demoralised by it. They speak as if the labouring 1 men would spend his days in idleness and rioting, and as if he would utile irn all the natural human instincts which now make him toil for his wife and family,because at the age of sixty-five or seventy he had the prospect,if he lived so long.of receiving this small annuity from the State. If the Bill really - answered to their description of it, and if, as they suggested, it illustrated in its history, and would illustrate in its operations, the worst and most dangerous tendencies of democracy, then surely it is precisely sue% a Bill that a Second Chamber exists to protect us from. But the value of and necessity for a Second Chamber is not often made strikingly app irent by the actions of the Lords. They could, almost at any time, bo convicted of such incompatibility of temper as to render imperative their divorce from the governing institutions of the land. Though, as a body, the Lords feared ,to adopt wrecking tactics towards the Pensions Bill, some of them voted in the minority against it. These_few peers who thus earned distinction were : _ _ _</p>
        <p>...... ....,---. ....-.............._ ._ _ . Abarcorn, Duke of, Mar, Earl of. Amherst, Earl. Mowbray, Lord. Avebury, Lord. Muskerry, Lord. Belhaven and Stenton, Lord. Newton, Lord. Carnwath, Earl of Northumberland, Duke of. Fingal!, Earl of. Stanmore, Lord. Forester, Lord. ' Wemyss, Earl of, Hardwicke, Earl of. Zouche of Haryngworth, Lord.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>--- Mach interest was aroused last evening in Sankey- street and Bridge-street owing to the presence of a parrot on the top of Trinity Church tower. The bird was whistling very contentedly, some say to the tune of " Cock o' the North." * * * Scarcity of employment, particularly at local iron- works, is given as the canoe of the Union out-relief rising by :tl5l during the sixteen weeks ending July 18th. The facts should come like rain upon a parched and barren land to the local Tariff Reformers.</p>
        <p>* * * There was a little bit of chaff at the Parr Hall luncheon on Thursday with regard to advertising— particularly in the soap trade, and Mr. Crostield gave a new version of an old couplet which caused much amusement. Said he " Early to bed and early to rise, is no good if you don't advertise."</p>
        <p>The movement for prescrAing the portrait of Mr. Lloyd-George to the Incorporated Law S)ciety has met with great success. Many Conservatives have subscribed, and have thus expressed their disapproval of the action of the society in refusing to place the portrait of Mr. Lloyd-George on its walls. • Englishmen were too confident about the great , Marathon race, and it is a fitting illustration of pride ,going before a fall that an English runner was not found, in the first three. The winner was Dorando, an Italian, whom apparently no one had taken into ! account in working out prognostications. Hayes (U.S.A.) was second, and Heffron (South Africa) was third. Up to now an Englishman has never won this ! event. Surely John Ball is not getting shortwinded • No time has been lost in getting to work at the new Post Office. It is the fashion with some public build- 1 im-js to formally declare them open and then close them for a time while the finishing touches are completed. Not so with the new Post Office, however. In a few hors after the Postmaster-General had opened it, business was in full swing and the first impressions of those who have already made use of it are decidedly favoulable. As Mr. Buxton said, Warrington is fortunate in having a Post Office which , can be matched by very few in up-to-dateness and general convenience. * * * Tariff Reformers long promised " work for all " under their visionary scheme, but recently they have changed their ground somewhat and after innumerable variations, " Wolk for All "has become merely "More Work "—a very much safer if less alluring promise. We hope now that" Higher Wages" will be taken in band, so that eventually we may get (it only means an alteration of two words)— TARIFF REFORM MEANS MORE WORK • FOR LESS WAGES.</p>
        <p>For full details see the recent Blue-book on the con- ditions of labour in Germany. * *</p>
        <p>In the course of his interesting reply to the con- gratulatory speeches of the Guardians on Wednesday, Mr. J. C. Sutton gave numerous personal recollections which will be read with interest by the present generation. For instance, few people know that Mr. Sutton was born in the familiar building now used as an office by Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., or that the building was at one time a Warrington mansion of historic impoitance, and subsequently the first Warrington Infirmary. We hope it will long be pre- served as a sample of old Warrington. Mr. Sutton haiks back to some early days when Mr. F. Monks and the late Mr. R. Garnett wee his schoolfellows.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
