o the Assessment Committee.</p>
        <p>The second annual open-air demonstration was held by the Newton Division Labour party on Saturday afternoon on the famous meeting ground near Haydeck Park race-course, and on the borders of the townships of Ashton-in-alakerfield and Elaydock. The procession included the miners's lodges from Garswood, Elaydock, Go!borne, Bryan Hall, Park-lane, and Collies Green ; glass-bottle makers, engineers, waggon-builders, &amp;a—, from Earlestown, and a mixed contingent representing different trades in St. Helens. The gathering numbered about 6,000—a smaller number than last year, due to the fact that the date clashed with the liayclock colliery excursion to Blackpool and other local events. Interesting speeches were made from two platforms, Mr. W. Speakman, chairman of the party, presided at No. I. and Mr. T. Watson, of the Engine Winders' Society, at No. 2 platform. The speakers included Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. for Newton •, Mr. 'l'. Glover, M.P. for St. lieleds ; Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P. for Ince ; and Mr. G. N. Barnes, M.P. Mr. Watson, speaking at No. 2 platform, said their opponents had recently stated that their member for the Newton Division (Mr. J. A. Seddon) was shortly destined to go to Orkney or Shetland. Without doubt their opponents desired that that might come to pass, but they the electors of Newton Division, had shown, by their appearance there that afternoon, and by the enthusiasm and the enirit of the Labour movernentahat they were all going Co stand together to retai a the Newton Division. (Applause). Dr. Lathorn moved a resolution expressisg entire confidence in the Labour party in Parliament, con- gratulating it on the union of the Labour forces in the House of Commons, and warmly appreciating the services rendered to the cause by the miners of Great Britain in their recent vote. Further, the resolution viewed with pleasure the introduction of an Old-age Pensions Bill and a Miners Eight Hours Bill, cordially approved of the valuable work of the Labour party with respect to both these measures, urged the necessity fel., an Unemployed Bill, and declared determination to preserve our Free Trade system, instead of "broadening the basis of taxation " by which the workers would be taxed on everything they used, and advocated increased taxation of the very wealthy. Mr. E. Radford, of Buttonwood, seconded, and Mr. Joseph Parkinson, of Wigan, supported. Mr. T. Glover, M.P. for St. Helens, said that daring the past two and a half years, whenever a measure was brought forward to benefit the workers, they had found the landowners and the capitalists dead set against it. These said that Labour wanted to rob them of that I which belonged to :them. They wanted to do nothing of the kind, but they wanted the working people, who were the producers of the wealth, to receive a fairer share of the wealth they produced. In reviewisg the labour legislation of the past two and a half years the speaker said that much more could have been done if they had had 200 or MO Labour representatives in the House of Commons instead or forty or fifty. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P. for Ince, said that the real inner work of the Labour Party in Parliament arose from the fact that only during recent years. had the working people outside Parliament realised their industrial necessities and determined to avail them- selves of their political power.—(Cheers.) Two years ago the people, pressed down by their industrial neces- sities, woke up to the fact that they had non-a political lever at their disposal, a weapon by which they might bring about their industrial salvation.—(Applause.) To realise how difficult it would be to achieve that indus- trial salvation they needed only to consider for one moment the conditions of their life. Although they were called freeborn Britons, and although on occasions they were willing to indulge in a little flag-waving, not at single inch of the land—the land of their birth— belonged to them. All those who desired to exercise , their labour for the maintenance of themselves and their families were prevented from doing so unless they were willing to sell that labour on conditions favourable to some other person. Two years ago their people resolved to return to Parliament men who knew their wants because they had worked and lived with them. Did the working man in the pit or on the sur- face think that he did not know the necessities of his own condition better than a double-first man from Cambridge University ?—(No.) But it was at one time thought that the work of the House of Commons and the House of Lords could only be performed by people who had gassed through the various colleges. There bad been too much cant about the whole thing. (Cheers.) For tOO or COO years every class of the corn- munity had been urging its own special claim to get into the House of Commons and to dominate, but when the class which in the trueet sense was no class but the nation—the working class—sought to have its wants attended to directly by sending to Parliament men of their own rank, those people were setting up the claim that they ought to attend to great Imperial interests, and of those interests the working class knew nothing. (Laughter.) The truest Imperial interest WRS to see how they could improve the condition of the people at home, which was the centre of the Empire. If they saw that the heart of the Empire was properly fed, that ' good rich blood was given it, they could leave the extremities to take care of themselves. (Cheers). Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. for the Newton division, ' referring to the Miners Eight Hours Bill,said he had no very great hope that they were going to get very much help unless they outside gave it to them. The Tory party almost to a man voted against the bill, but the LORD NEWTON ON COMPULSORY MILITARY , member for Ormskirk did not do so, as he knew there SERVICE, I were enough colliers in Ormskirlt to turn him out. • Referring to the position of the Labour party in the Speaking in the House of Lords on Thursday Lord Newton said it was the view of (ho National Service New ton division,Mr Seddon said they were doubly as i League that it should be the duty of every yo strong as they man , Aarnesre t the last general election. w M.P. also spoke of the need for to train for the purposes of national defence. They G. N. working men to be represented in Parliament by men proposed to do with men very much the same as the or their own class who knew their needs, who had lived Government hoped to do with the Territorial force, their lives and were therefore in a better position to The difference between the Government's plan and that voice their aims rind aspirations than Lord Newton or any theirs would succeed, because they would enforce efficiency and make preparation for war before war capitalist or landlord could possibly do. It was owing , to the presence in Parliament of a strong and able body commenced. The Government did not propose that of Labour representatives that they had at last brought serious preparation should take place until war broke the questions of old-age pensions and miners' eight out. He was convinced that a voluntary system was hours from the region of the clouds and abstraction not sufficient for our requirements, and that universal into the area of practical politics. (Cheers). service for home defence was necessary. The resolution WES unanimously.</p>
