        <p>A GIFT.</p>
        <p>The dried currant stands for all time as a type of Nature's richest gifts to mankind.</p>
        <p>Here we have neither fibre, seed, nor stone, but a little ball of purest nutriment, which, as soon as ever the fine skin of the fruit is broken, goes to the nourish- ing of the body.</p>
        <p>I Housewives will find an unusual display of tempting Currant Bread and Currant Confections in the bakers' windows. Sure proof, this, that the bakers are moving ' with the times, and have rhien to the popular demand for cakes enriched with this wonderfully sustaining fruit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Farmer's Action Falls.</head>
        <p>Farmer's Action Falls.</p>
        <p>His Honour-Judge Shiress, Will, K.C. heard an action at the Wallington County Court on Thursday in which Edwin Fairclough, farmer, Burtonwood, sued J. H. Berry, corn dealer, St. Helens, for S:5O damages for breach of contract, his contention being that through the omission of defendant to supply hitn with certain oats in exchange for others, one of his fields had to remain out of cultivation for a season. Mr. E. A. Whits appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. Morris, barrister (instructed by Mr. Fox, St. Helens) for the defendant.— Plaintiff and one of his witness's in cress-examination admitted that oats could have been obtained elsewhere, probably at a cheaper price then defendant offered to supply them, and that there was time to sow them.—llis Efonour held that plaintiff should have obtained oats from some other source and sued for any difference in price. He gave judgment for the defendant.</p>
        <p>IF THE EYES have a tired sensationjf troubled with habitual headache, if the eyes cannf6t stand the light, get the sight tested by JOHNSTON WATSON, Eyesight Specialist, 81, Bridge-street, Warrington. Low charges. Satisfaction guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
