      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. lIELENS COLLIERY FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Frederick Tiplady (39), engineer, of 20, Waterloo- street, Bolton, met with a shocking death at St. Helens on Monday. Tiplady's family resided in Edin- burgh. For about two years he had been employed by Messrs. John Booth and Son, construction engineers, who for some time have been erecting a new and up-to- date iron girder headgear at the Sutton Manor Collieries. On Monday a girder strut weighing about 17 cwt. was being raised, and when the girder was within an inch of being placed in its proper position deceased stepped oil on to it, upon which the chain ',suddenly broke, and he fell with the girder a distance of 02 feet t 3 the ground. When picked up he was found to have sustained fractures of the arm and thigh, and he died soon after being taken to the hospital.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS MINERS' STRI[iE. THE MEN "DEFIANT."</p>
        <p>The miners on strike at Ashton Green Colliery, St Helens, held a meeting cu Monday afternoon on the Parr Football Ground to consider the position of affairs. Mr. Parkinson, miners' agent, addressed the meeting, and said that there were still three men defiant and who refused to join the federation. One of these is said to be an advanced trade union, a member of the Industrial Unionist of the World,aud for some personal reason he declined to join the federation. Mr. Twist addressed the meeting, and said the federa- tion officials had approached the Coal Owners' Defence Association, and also representatives of the Ashton Green Colliery, and asked them to guarantee them in 1 futute that all union men should be employed at the pits, but for the present this guarantee had been refused. Under these circumstances, he said, there seemed to be nothing for it but that the struggle must continue. The men were asked to vote if they were willing to return to work on the present conditions, but they decided not to resume work until every man is a mem- ber of the Miners' Federation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF A £5 NOTE,</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday before Mr. F. A. Frost, a Londoner named Arthur Flint, was charged with stealing a .C 5 note from Walter 1 Longbotham (Bingley), at Ilaydock l'ark Races on ' Saturday, July llth.—Mr. Browne appeared for the prisoner.—Evidence was given to the effect that Detective-Sergeant Sutton's suspicions were aroused by the movement of a gang in the shilling ring just before the second race, and he bad Vim watched. The gang surrounded a man who was raaking a bet, and it was allegod that Flint took a purse from the man's hip pocket. The officer at pace arrested Flint, and the rest of the gang badly mauied the °facer in attempting to release the accused, but he stuck to his prisoner until he received assistance. The purse was lost in the struggle. Prisoner was taken to the racecourse police station and £ll in gold and a £3 note were found in his possession. The latter was claimed by Longbothain, who said it had been taken from his pocket during the afternoon.—Prisouer was -remanded to the Newton Petty Sessions. LABOUR DEMONSTRATION AT HAYDOCK. LOCAL M.P.'S SPEECHES. GOVERNMENT BILLS APPROVED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Athlete's Success.</head>
        <p>Athlete's Success.</p>
        <p>At the Salford Harriers Sports at Belle Vue on Saturday, L. C. Daintith, of Warrington, off the 33 yards mark won the quarter mile bicycle race in 31 3.5 seconds. Death of Mrs. Joseph. - - - - - -</p>
        <p>Our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Joseh, wife of Dr. G. VV. Joseph, 21, Bald-street, who passed away on Wednesday. The funeral will take place to-day (Saturday. The cartge will leave Bold-street at twelve o'clock and proceed to Holy Trinity Church where the first part of ths burial service will be held. The interment will take place at the Cenactery at one o'clock. The Friends' School. The scholars of the Warrington Friends' School had a picnic to Framley, Cheshire, on Thursday, and as ' the weather was all that could be desired the visit was a complete success. The party numbering about twenty-five, and under the care of Mrs. Ilowlinson and Miss Taylor, left in one of Nlr. Connor's buses at 2.30. On arriving at Framley, tea was served and after- wards games were indulged in. Warrington was ' reached at 0.30 after a very pleasant time.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—AdVt. Apollo Choir's Success.</p>
        <p>-The Warrington Apollo Male Choir - (conductor Mr. Harry Berrey) was successful in winning -first prize value .:Cl.O oe Wednesday at Connah's Quay Eisteddfod. The adjudicators were Messrs. E. D. Lloyd, R.A.M. and John Henry, R.A.M. The test piece was " Crossing the Plains" (Price). Six choirs competed. The adjudicators in awarding the prize to the Apollo Choir said the choir was splendidly trained, and gave a magnificent rendering from beginning to end. Master Arthur Greenough of Willis-street gained let prize in the pianoforte section, the piece being " Hungarian Dance." PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER TIIR CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASIIIRE.—Advt. Dispute About an Insurance Policy. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C.; Susannah Connor, 6, Owen-street, sued Mary Flynn, 4, Naylor- street, for the specific return of a policy of insurance on the life of Mary Mahoney in the Prudential Assurance Company Ltd.—Mr. Browne appeared for the plaintlf and Mr. tinsworth for the defendant.— Plaintiff's case was that Mrs. Mahoney insured herself in 1:332 for £ll, A few years later she went to reside in Ireland and left the policy with the mother of the plaintiff and defendant. The mother kept up the premiums for a number of years but for eigdit years plaintiff had paid the premiums herself. Shortly before the mother's death in August, 1905, plaintiff asked the defendant to keep-the policy until such time as she required it. When Mrs. Mahoney died in March last, defendant refused to give the policy up. —Defendant's case was that the policy had been in , her possession for 17 years with the exception of twelve months when she left it with a neighbour to keep for her. Up to the time of the mother's death she paid half the premium.—ln cross-examination defendant admitted that she had not paid any premiums since her mother's death in 1905.—His Honour held that plaintiff had failed to make out her case and gave judgment for the defendant with costs. For Sale, cheap, 2Sinch Grtt:EN's PONY LAWN MoavErt, good condition, suit cricket club,— GARLAND, 31, Bridge et., Warrington,—Advt. ZS 12s. for a Horse Bite. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, KG., Martha Cannon, 2, Talbot-yard, King-street, sued Messrs. Naylor and Co., confectioners, &amp;c., Queen-street, for 5,12 damages for injuries alleged to have been sustained in cansepence of a bite by the defendants' horse on July Ist, 1907. -Mr. Walton, barrister (instructed by Mr. Arthur Fos) appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. G. N. Goldie, barrister (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) represented the defendants.— Plaintiff's case was that the horse was standing cut- side the defendant's shop in Queen-street. Plaiutilll was looking into the shop windaw when the horse turned routed and bit her on the right arm, inflicting a wound for which she had to be medically attended. Plaintiff called a man named Darns, formerly in the employ of the defendant, who stated that he had been told that the horse bit someone at Runcorn, and another witness stated that the animal had snapped at her.—Defendant stated that plaintiff's little boy touched the harse with a stick and it was startled and turned round, its bead knocking against plaintiff's arm. It did not, however, bite her. Witnesses were called to state that the animal was lot vicious.—After a lengthy hearing his Honour gave jildgment for the plaint;jf f3r 12s. and casts,</p>
        <p>BABY CAIIRLAGES Of every des.,:ription.—J. S. Gwnt..A.No,sl to 55,Bridge st.,Warrington.—Advt. Children's Summer Camp. A detachment of girls, to the number of OS, left f .)1. a holiday at the children' summer camp at Prestatyn on Saturday morning. The roll-call having been called, the girls formed two-deep and marched with their officers to Bank Quay Station. Mrs. Wilkinson, superintendent-in-charge, and her assist- ants—the Misses Ercson, Birah,Bottoniley,lfcGeach'n, Pennington and Whitely, Mr. Roberts and other ' edrication department officials, Messrs. Bamber, Ifol- ' brook, Rutter and Farrar were in attendance. The Landon and North-Western Railway Company had, at the request of the hon. secretary, very kindly pro- vided a corridor carriage and other separate compart- ments for the party.As the 10.1 S a.m. departed merry r voices were raised in joyful acclamation at the prospect 'of a week's holiday at the seaside. Many of the ' little ones had not seen the sea. The weather was threatening. As the train neared Chester the clouds dispersed and the sunshine augured well for a bright summer's day. Nor were those hopes dispelled. The camp revelled in brightness and the fresh north-west breeze swept the incoming tide on the beach with a mighty roar of welcome. With hearty laughter the new camp.dwellers paddled in the foamy breakers until the welcome tea-bell summoned the hungry children to their first meal. On Sunday it was fine and all the children went to church as usual. Bathing in the sea was enjoyed in the afternoon, and all retired to rest tired. but pleased, with the events of the day. The children, looking much better for their splendid holiday, returned to Warrington last evening at eight o'clock. Mr. and Mrs. Whiting are the camp cooks and caretakers, and are assisted by Miss E. Bostcck.</p>
        <p>PADGATE. COTTAGE HOMES ROSE FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>Quite an interesting event took place last Thursday, both afternoon and evening at Padgate, when the Rose Queen Festival in connection with the Cottage Homes was held. There were present at each of the coronations about five hundred people, and amongst those who attended were—the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman Smethurst and Mrs. Smethurst), Councillor Ashcroft, Mr. IL Bolton, Mr. W. Gill, Mr. W. Furness, Mr. AV. C. Brooks, Colonel Buckton, Alderman Bennett, Father I Whittle, Rev. G. W. Dickinson (chaplain), and Mr. A. Wright (superintendent). The Queen this year is Miss Sarah O'Brien and her Lord Chancellor is Master Arthur Longshaw, who by the way is the brother of last year's Queen. Much praise is due to the teachers for the admirable way in which they trained the scholars. All. the children, numbering 207, took part in the programme, which was very much enjoyed by the visitors. The order of procession was as follows : Band, morris dancers, garland girls fishermen, pierrots, clowns, Darby and Joan, Hump&amp; Dumpty, seasons, Minnie Palmer, darners, laundry maids, tailors, butchers, shoeruakers,Buffalo Bill and cow bovs.Lancashire lad and lass,farruers and boy blue,John Bull, Irishman and girls, scrubbers, chefs, Bo Peep, sailors, Jack Horner, Jack and Jill, boys in navy blue, niggers, La Poupee and Dutch girl, nurse, Welsh girls, Scotch girl and boy, shoe-blacks, Home Notes, Woman's Life, painters, Britannia Poppie, Topsy, flower girls, Japs, two little girls, milkmaids in blue, Red Riding Hood, lancers children, Puck, old woman in shoe, gipsy queen, pack of cards, rose queen, jockeys, train clearers, maids of honour, guards of honour, Lord Chamberlain, attendant. In the evening the Mayor and Mayoress presented the prizes to the best children. Tho recipients numbered about thirty-five, and the Mayor shook hands with each one of them. _</p>
        <p>After the prize distribution Mr. E. L. Bent , (chairman) proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor. He was sure all thanks were due to him for putting himself to inconvenience to present the prizes. Mr. R. Bolton said he had very great pleasure in seconding the vote of thanks. They were going to give the children a day or two's outin,, at the seaside. Mr. W. Furness heartily supported the resolution. At this stage the Rose Queen and train-bearers pre- sented bouquets to the Mayoress, Mrs. Bolton and Mts. Bent, and a button-hole to the 11fayor. The Mayor in replying said that he rose to thank them for the kind welcome which had been given that day to himself and his dear 'wife. He could assure everyone on the field that they were both delighted to be amongst them once more. As a speaker had said, it had been a great inconvenience to them to go there that afternoon, but they were repaid in the beautiful Rose- Queen festival by those dear children. A display of rockets concluded a very bright and interesting day.</p>
      </div>
