      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington went dowiTa-.t-E--a-;lestown on Saturday in a League engagement. They were handicapped by the indisposition of A. Duckworth their wicket keeper, and If. Callon and Earlestown, cc-110 are going strong for the championship, strained every nerve to win. The match was somewhat exciting, Warrington making strenuous efforts to avert defeat, but Earlestown managed to win by 66. Earlestown were the first to go to the wicket and amassed 159, Morris giving a brilliant display for his 46. Warrington were all got out for 112, the top scorer being J. W. Bibby, who knocked up 41 in his best style. Cresswell took saved wickets for 29 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd suffered their first defeat of the season on Saturday when Earlestown 2nd piled on a majority of 103 with five wickets to spare. Warrington batted in indifferent fashion, their best effort being that of A. Thewlis who made 22. The Earlestown men carried everything before them and F. Valentine, who hit out most vigorously, placed the substantial figure of 81 to his credit.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Le)gue Cheshire Lines Engineers encountered Bewsey Weileyans and bad to admit defeat by 15. S. Millett (19) and G. Chamber- lain (13) batted creditably for their respective sides.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recs. had not much difficulty in defeating Walker's Brewery, who only made 31 as compared with the winners' CL G. McGowan batted in fine style for his 22 runs.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Junior League engagement between Orford and St. James's resulted in a very easy victory for Orford, for whom Sandy took seven wickets for fiveruns and S. Leigh three for six. J.Bennett was the top scorer with 22 to his credit.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists administered a severe drubbing on Garswood Hall 2nd, who were all out for 42 in response to Latchford's 133. For the home team A. E. Walker and James Cox batted well for their scores of 43 and 30. E.Clifte bowled effectively for the Baptists, taking five wickets for 12 runs.</p>
        <p>-- A match between Ashley 2nd and Grappeahall 2nd resulted in a draw in favour of Grappenhall. Toulinin (12) and Woodward (16) did splendid service with the bat for the victors.</p>
        <p>Warrington Police failed aga:nst the Salford custodians of the peace, the latter amassing 107 far 7 wickets against Warrington's kB. P.C. Berry was in great form with the bat and made a splendid 4,5 for Warrington.</p>
        <p>Last evening Walker's No. 1 mot the Post Oflice the Works amlpetitionlsemi-finai and defeated them by 67 to 24.</p>
        <p>For the semi-final on Tuesdly in the Works Cricket Competition the following players have been selected to represent Pearson and Knowles, against the War- rington Corporation on the Arpley Cricket Ground : Mr. James Dodd; J. Thompson, S. Partridge, A. Warhurst, J. Toulmin, A. Tarbocic, J.Jones, W.l3ennett, B. Bun, R. Tanner, C.Trm,</p>
        <p>Bowling. Warrington has done well in getting, two teams irt the semi-finals of the Lancashire and Cheshire club championship. There should be a good prospect of first honours coming to the old town, although two close matches are anticipated, whatever the draw. The teams left in are :—Warrington Bull's Head, Unsworth Conservative, Whitefleld, and IVarrington Cemetery. The match between Warrington Cemetery and Hazel Grove, which was played on the green attached to the Lord Nelson Hotel, Urmston, was well worth going a long way to see, there being some exceptionally close bowling. This was the case in the game between T. Ellison and A. Mali-ins in particular, as end after end they were beating one another within inches. A. Scott also played a good game, and it only took him 18 ends- to pol;sh off his opponent, J. Smith, who scored 6 whilst Scott went to game. Scott's score was a bit out of the ordinary, it reading five doubles, one single, and then five more doubles. A favourite with the Hazel Grove people was T. Bennett, and there is no doubt about him being a trier all the way, but be had no ' chance with T. Harrison. who fully deserved his win, 21-12. Young Gleave, who is cousin to the well- known Albert of that name, bowled very well indeed against J. Wolfenden, who, however, after a keen bout, proved just a bit too good. They peeled at 3, 4,6, 8, 11, 17 and IS, and at 19 and 21 Glees° had the jack, but played two very bad woods. Torn Bolton was too good altogether for J. Barber, one of the stars of the Hazel Grove team, and won 21-13. G. Ball, senior, piled on a few more points towards the total majority of 67 for Warrington. It was a treat to see this hardy veteran miming across the green after his woods.</p>
        <p>There was a most exciting finish to the match between Warrington Bull's Head and Didsbury, the former team gaining a victory by the small margin of 4 points. Each team SuCtaLlee.l. 111 winning sig games, but the losers were badly let down by A. E. Smith, who only registered 4 chalks against E. Shaw, and Tom Sykes, who was defeated to the tune of 21-7 by J. Jennings, making a total deficit of 31 points between them.</p>
        <p>Another game which told against Didsbury was that in which H. Arnold, jun., and A. Weave were the participants. They were well up with one another at the start, and at the fourteenth end Arnold led 30-7. From this point, however, be never scored a single chalk, the Bull's Head captain running right away to game—a really marvellous performance against a player of the calibre of Arnold.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Works Competition makes gnod pro- gress and interest is as keen as ever, On Saturday London and North-Western and Whitecross No. 2 emerged triumphant from the second round, whilst Whitecross No. I were victorious in the third round.</p>
        <p>There was a close encounter between London and North-Western and Crosfhld's No. 1, the railwaymen winning by 13 points. One of the best games was between J. Jackson and J. Millington. Millington started off by notching a couple but Jackson soon over- took him and led 5-3. The pla3er9 were level at 6-6. 12-12 and 14-n, and Jackson bowling the winning wood triumphed by 15-11.</p>
        <p>Whitecross No. 2 accounted for Castle Rubber by the substantial majority of 59. There was a good game between A. Field and Az--Thornley. Field had a long run of succesPes and led 8-3. His opponent, however, crept up and at 13 the players were on equal terms. Field then got a couple and won by 15-13.</p>
        <p>A third round encounter between Whitecross No. I and Crosileld's No. 2 ended in a victory for the wire men by 25 points. A keen game was witnessed between P. Wright and J. Shaw. Wright made a good start and had the advantage by 6-1. Shaw forged ahead and the players were level at 11 and 13. Shaw then notched one but Wright had the good fortune to follow it with a couple and won by 15-14.</p>
        <p>To-day's matches are : Whitecross No. 1 v. P.</p>
        <p>Whitecross No. 2 v. Rs lands' No. 1. L.&amp; N.-W. v. Monks, Hall No. 1.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. A meeting in connection with the newly-formed Warrington and District Junior Rugby League(aitiliated to the Northern Union) will be held on the IVarrington Football ground next Wednesday at 7.30 p.m. Mr. H. Ashton will preside, and all the clubs are invited to send representatives. The business will include the appointment of committee, Ir.c. Up to the present eight clubs have joined and others wishing to join are requested to send a representative to the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING. – Gray--driffishaw._</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING. - Gray--driffishaw._</p>
        <p>There was a pretty wedding at St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, on Tuesday, when Mr. James Gray (assistant engineer in the Warrington Corporation Water Department), son of the late Mr. Thomas Gray, was married to Miss Evelyn Hannah Grimshaw, daughter of Mr. B. Gritnshaw, of Gate Wharf,Sankey. The Rev. J. It. Jones (vicar) officiated. The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in a dress of white crepe de chine in the Empire style, trimmed with white lace and orange blossom, tulle veil with orange blossom and white heather, and carried a bouquet of hhes of the valley and white heather, the gift of the bridegroom. She also wore a diamond and sapphire ring, the present of the bridegroom. She was attended by two brides- maids—Miss Emily Grimshaw (sister of the bride) and Miss Gwendoline Sankey, who wore pretty dresses of pink muslin over white silk, Empire style, trimmed with cream lace, and pink hats trimmed with lace and feathers, and carried bouquets of sweet peas. Their ornaments were pearl pendants, the gifts c.f the bridegroom. Mr. John Teller, (tramways manager) I discharged the duties of best man and Mr. Prank A. Milling acted as groomsman. Mr. Randall, organist of Holy Trinity Church, Warrington, was at the organ and played appropriate selections. After the ceremony a reception was held at Gate Wharf, the W inmarlergh Cafe Company having charge of the catering. Later the happy couple left for Loudon and the South Coast-, the blide wearing a heliotrope costume and bat to match. The presents, which were numerous and valuable, included a canteen of cutlery from tha officials of the Corporation and a handsome pair of silver flower vases, the gift of the employees of the Water Committee.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
