      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>These teams met at the St. Helens Baths, on Tues- day night, in the first division of the Liverpool League. St. Helens played from the deep end, and J. Barr immediately dribbled up to the Warrington goal, but was roblyed. St. Helens still attacked, and T. Gibbons , scored it fine goal in spite of the attentions °flare Warringtonians. J. Barr next shot wide, but A. Barr received shortly afterwards and scored the second for the home team. Immediately on resuming Pemberton notched a point for Warrington. Ashton and Gibbons were then ordered oat for fouling. Half-time : St. , Helens 2 goals, Warrington 1. In the second half St. I Helens bad easily the latst of the play. Heaton (2), , Barr, and Burrows scored for the home team, who won easi, by 6 goals to I. A second team match between St. Helens and Toxteth produced a great struggle. At half-time there was no score. Bryant and Dolan scored for Toxteth, and Lomax and Pcnaington for St. Helens. , Result : Two goals each.</p>
        <p>Tragic Death of an Old' Warringtonian. On Monday evening a tragic incident occurred on the howling green of the Upper Clapton Club in the course of the final for the All England Shield between the home team and Carlisle. Mr. H. Grounds, a well- known manufacturer,cf Tottenham' who was captaining the 'home club, dropped dead. The game was abandoned. Mr. Grounds is a native of Warrington, and his numerous friends here will hear of his death with much regret. Ho was in business in Warrington luntil be left for London where he had secured an important appointment. One of his last visits to War- IringLn was about 12 months ago, when he gave the I Mayor a cheque for 5:10 for the Infirmary Extension ' Fund.</p>
      </div>
