        <head>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., and the. Postmen.</head>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., and the. Postmen.</p>
        <p>THE BOROUGH MEMBER GIVES A LUNCHEON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At a general meeting of the Warrington branch , of the Postmen's Federation held at Friars Green i House on Sunday, atily leth, 1908, the following . i resolution was unammou,sly adopted.— "That the best thanks of this meeting of the Warrington Branch Postmen's Federation be sent to ....._ A. 11. Crostield. Esq , M.P., for the prompt manner in ' 1 which he voiced the injustice being done to Warrington , The Right Honourable Sydney Buxton, M.P., Post- postmen in reducing master Geoeral, visited Warrington on Thursday to the rates of pay at that ()Mee, and • also for the further efforts he made on open the new General Post Office in Springfield-street. our behalf to have this injarAice removed." ".1). .1. e building, which has cost 5,16,000, is one of the It was also decided to send a copy of the above to most modern o a sin e country, and is a great i f 1 kind ' th the local papers and I trust you will, give it due you acquisition to the town, as supplying not only a much 1 prominence in this week's " Exarainer." Thanking in anticipation also for the way in which YOU brought needed extension of postal accommodation but a our cause before the public last week. On behalf of , pleasing architectural feature. Great interest was the above branch I am faithfully yours. i taken in the opening and ceremony, favoured with -JAMES R. OXLEY. 1 beautiful weather, the function passed off most Jaly 23, 1908. , successfully. The Postmaster General motored to 1 Warrington from Liverpool, accompanied by the Hon. Testimonial to Councillor Stott. iR. D. Denman (private secretary), Mr. F. Salisbury Sir,—May I through the medium of your valuable (Postmaster of Liverpool and Surveyor of the district), paper announce the fact that a testimonial to Conn- and 11r. Cowhey (one of the principal clerks at oilier Fred Stott, 3.P., is being obtained. On behalf of Liverpool). On arriving here about noon he immed- the committee appointed to carry out this object, I iately proceeded to the old Post Ooffice which he would like to invite all friends and well-wishers of inspected with interest. Afterwards he was conducted j Councillor Stott to contribute to the same. I feel sure there are many who, although perhaps differing to the new building whore he addressed a meeting of I from him in his political convictions, yet admire and employees in the sorting room. 1 _r. B _ letton was presented with an address of wel- , respect him for his work and services to the town, M cpme .on. behalf of the Postal and Telegraph Clerks' ' and for his own personal character. We hope by the , asoorciation y .. ter,. ePostmaster. b Mr. Lis' the b help of all our friends to make him a substantial M . BUY-TON in returning thanks for the address, testimeni a 1 . , t alluded to the recognition of the Postal Clerks' essocin- Contributions may be maid in to the "Stott and' ' ' tion . .the Postmen's Federation, and said that such Teseamonial Fund" at tha National and Provincial Bank, recognition of the two unions had proved an enormous Bridge-street, or to myself.. I beg to remain yours , incerely, advantage to a great administrative department of that s JOSEPH PODMORE, jun., treasurer, j sort, because it brought the officials and the ' heads together. and they were able -to 8, Rylands-street, Warrington. , thrash out questions in a very short time. July 23rd, 1903. During the whole time he had been Postmaster-General -- 1 he had received great kindness from all sections of the Garman and English Workers. ! staff, and he hoped that during the time he held that i Sir,—Those who champion any particular thought or position he would retain all their confidence. (Hear, hear). policy are always in danger of only seeing the facts : On the motion of Mr. Bedell, of the Postal and and figures which favour their own views. It is satis- . Telegre.ph Clerks' Association, seconded by Mr. J. R. factory therefore to be able to turn to facts and figures Oxley, secretary of the Postmen's Federation, a hearty compiled by disinterested parties, all the more so when vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Buxton. these are compiled and arranged by experts in the work, , who have experience and knowledge to protect them i AT THE TOWN HALL. against the many pit-fails in which inadequate data or ! faulty deduction land the unwary. I The meeting over, Mr. Buxton went to the Town Hall where he was received by the Mayor and The volume recently issued by the Board of Trade Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst). A large comprises between five and six hundred piges, and institutes comparisons between the condition of the company of prominent townspeople had assembled and working classes in England and Germany. This work ' the Mayor took the opportunity of introducing them is the second of a se:les, the first one was issued some to the Postmaster-General. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, MP. time ago and had reference to the conditions in America , was amongst those present. and England. The Board of Trade have in compilation , At about ten minutes past one a procession was a third volume instituting a Like comparison between formed, headed by the mace and sword bearers, and the conditions of the working classes- of France and England. The work before us is of an illuminating proceeded to the new Post Office. character and deals in great detail with the question. I All the sections are of interest to students of social and commercial questions, but only a summary of the main conclusions can be here given. First as to the standard and cost of living. After going into minute details in regard to rent, fuel and food, the three principal articles of expenditure, the compilers find that if an English workman emigrated to Germany and maintained the same standard of living as in this country he would find his emit of rent, fuel, and food raised one fifth. In other words an English workman paying 20s. in England for these three things would have to pay 21s. for the same in Germany. On the other hand, if a German workman emigrated to England and maintained his lower standar:Li i Yin g on less meat etc., he would be able to live in England on one tenth less than in Germany. Second, wages and hours. In wages, based upon a comparison of seventeen leading trades, the German workman receives 17 per cent. less than workmen of the same trades in England, while at the same time he works 10 per cent, longer hours. Putting the two facts together the hourly rate of wages is three quarters of the English, while the cost of rent, fuel, and food (measured by English standard) is about one fifth higher. Interesting detailed tables are given showing the difference in the price of food. Everything in common use is dearer with the exception of potatoes and milk, which are not directly affected by tariffs. The relation between the English and the German working man's budget is 100 to 113, that is, an Enelish workman can for Sc. 4d. procure as much as his German comrade for 9s.lod.,but as the German workman gets less, worlds longer hoursond when he has got his reduced money finds it has less spending power and has to be satisfied with less, he is trebly hit. If, however, we take the German working man's budget as the standard the comparison is as 103 to 109, that is, if 9s. be expended on articles upon Which a German lives the equivalent articles in England could be procured at Sc. 4d. Expressing the figures in percentages, and taking England as one hundred,the following figures are given : Wages Hours Labour 9, 100 ... If 111 Hourly Rate e 100 ... 91 75 ' Expenses in Food, rent and fuel „ 100 ... e 119 j In connection with rent, to make the comparison fair the taxes paid in the rants in England, but paid, separately in Germany, are in the case of the latter added to the rents. It is well to explain that this investigation has been in progress for three years and that the above figures are comparisons made on the figures as existing in October of 1905. There is no doubt that such com- parisons will vary somewhat from year to year. It might properly be contended that since the year 1903 -.German wages have advanced, but on the other hand the purchasing power has diminished. Some articles being unaffected by the new Tariff are practically the same as in 1905, while flour has advanced very consider- ably, and has raised the price of the home grown equivalents, such- as rye, out of which the black bread is made and which forms such an import- ant item in the German workingman's budget. It is impossible actually to say how the comparisons would be altered if 1903 were taken as the basis instead of 1905.A1l that we can say is that taking into account the rise in wages and the rise in cornmodities the comparison would be very little different to that, made in the Inquiry before us. And it is still true that the German workman (trotted out by our Tariff Reformers as the beneficiary of the German Tariff) has to work longer hours for lower weges, and when he has got his wages can buy considerably less. Figures are given showing that the consumption in late years of meat by the German working dames has diminished. This is now just about half of the amount consumed by English workmen. As the period in question synchronizes with the period of taxation of meat, &amp;c., it indicates that the German workman has not had the wherewithal to buy so much meat because of the higher general standard of prices. It is true that during recent years the consumption of horse flesh in Germany has increased, but not to the same extent as the diminution of the consumption of the meat, of cattle. Altogether the volume referred to is worthy of very careful consideration by all who take an interest in the well-being of our workers, and I would specially com- mend it to the Warrington Tariff Reformers who are so anxious to introduce a similar system of taxation into our own country.</p>
        <p>Peaketh, July 2lit, 1003.</p>
        <p>SUFFRAGETTES ON THE SCENE.</p>
        <p>a profit out of them. (Laughter and applause.) Members of the Post (Mice staff then appeared upon the scene and proceeded to do a brisk business in stamps which were purchased as souvenirs of the occasion. Alderman Tinnion claimed the proud distinction of having purchased the first stamp. The first telegram was one from the Mayor to the Postmaster-General congratulating him upon having opened the The visitors then made an inspection of the office. LUNCHEON AT THE PARR HALL i REFORMS IN THE POSTAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>At 1.39 Mr. A. H. Crostleld, M.P. entertained a large company consisting of members of the Town Council, prominent manufacturers and public men at luncheon in the Parr Hall. Mr. Crosfleld presided and had on his right the Postmaster General and On his left the Mayor of Warrington. The following bad accepted invitations to be present :—Aldermen Bennett, Bolton, Burton, Ellison, Evans Pierpoint, Tinnion ; Councillors Ashcroft, Ball, Barton, Benson, Cunnington, Dickenson, Evans, Fulton Smith, Hawthorn, Locker, Lord, Pemberton, Sankey, Singleton, Skelland, Stott, Toole, Wilkinson; Rev. W. Bracecanip(Mayor's chaplain), Hon. R. D. Denman (Mr. Buxton's private secretary), Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough, Captain G. R. Crosfleld, Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), G. F. la White (magistrates' clerk), C. Madeley (librarian), J. Deas (water engineer), F. V. L. Mathias (electrical engineer), J. Moore Murray (director of education), W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer), Revs. 'LP. Williamson, Father 'Wilson and John Yonge, Messrs. H. Kingdon (president of the Chamber of Commerce), F. Salisbury (Postmaster of Liverpool), W. Slingo (superintendent engineer), Dr. Bowden (medical officer at the Post Office), J. Cowhey (Liverpool), J. Crane, W. P. Rylands, Ziba Armitage, Dr. Smith, yore B. Davies,F. Monks, James Dodds' F. A. Frost, T. Birtles, S. Rigby Armitage, John White, A. L. Parkinson (builder of the new Post Office), E. A. Collis (clerk of works), J. Lister (postmaster of Warrington), H. C. Jones (chief clerk), J. H. Smethurst, T. Pemberton, Jos. Wilkinson, Felix Maginn, E. Ridgway, John Fairclough, A. Rathbone,R. E. Garnett, G. P.aliln, R. I. Wynne Jones, W. R. Hutchings, Edwin Woods, F. Pendlobury, J. J. Bleckly, W. H. Meekly, H. Greene% John Coxon, F. Kenyon, R. Chadwick' E. V. Walker. Mr. Powys catered in his best style. After luncheon, a short toast list was gone through. "HIS MAJESTY THE KING." , Mr. CROSFIELD said that in asking them to drink to the health of his Majesty the King one naturally felt in some difficulty as to whether one should dwell upon the work which the King did at home in our own country or upon what they equally admired —the influ- ence that his Majesty had exercised in so many of the great countries of Europe. (applause.) It seemed to THE BUILDING OPENED. him difficult to draw any satisfactory distinction betwen the two • they were so closely connected. One On arriving at the building, the Postmaster-General I could not reflect upon that Very true maxim of a former at one once opened the door which will ba used as the Lord Derby that " the greatest British interest is the public entrance, s vying : "I hive groat pleasure in interest of peace "—(applause)—without remembering declaring the new post office open:" what a great part the King had played upon the Conti' The company than entered the building and there , nent of Europe—a part which had earned him not in was a little speech-making in the general office, one quarter alone but in so many quarters the honoured title of Edward the Peacemaker. (Applause.) In the THE MAYOR'S WELCOME. House of Commons the otaer day they had a mernor- , able debate on the King's visit to the Czar, a The MAYOR. said :—lt gives me very great pleasure indeed on behalf of the inhabitants of Warrington to debate in which Sir Edward Grey made one thank you Mr. Postmaster-General for having come of the finest speeches be ever delivered in down from London to open this new Post 0 lice. We his life. He (Hr. Crosaeld) for his part must say that all know what great demands the nation makes upon considering how splendidly the King's intluence had ' your time, and therefore we appreciate this visit the been exercised in countries like France, Spain, Italy ; , more. The very handsome and commodious building and others on the Continent, and considering the state o which you have just opened, sir, while being a fitting of that unhappy Empire of Russ