      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CHARLES E. PARKER.</head>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>JEWELLER'S SHOP BROKEN INTO.</p>
        <p>Smart Police Capture at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Thursday, before Mr. John Fairclough and other magistrates.—James Higgins, 7, Naylor-street, was charged with breaking and entering the lock-up shop of Samuel Fernley jeweller, 71, Bridge-street, between Friday night and Saturday morning, and stealing a quantity of gold bracelets, a necklet, gold alberts and brooches of the value of £3O. James Ready and Elizabeth Ready, T, Naylor-street, were charged with receiving the property, knowing it to have been stolen. Superintendent Monaghan stated that the premises were left secure by Mr. Fernley on Friday night, and on opening tbe shop on Saturday morning he discovered that the shop had been entered. On an examination being made it was found that an entrance had been effected by climbing over a high wall at the rear of the premises and breaking a window on the ground floor. The police were informed, and he (Superintendent 31onashan) and Inspector Williamson made inquiries and visited Ready's house. Higgins made a statement, and the three prisoners were arrested. On searching the house some of the stolen jewellery was found in one of the spouts, and it was afterwards ascertained that the female prisoner had disposed of the bracelets to differ- ent women. When charged Higgins said the ievvellery was given to him by 3 man he did not know. The other prisoners said that Rigging brought the jewellery to the house. Prisoners were remanded until to-day.</p>
        <p>ROMAN TRIPOD UNEARTHED IN MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>During excavations between Brazenose-etreet and Qneen-stroet, Manqhester. a small tripod was found in a cavern which is believed to be art ancient grave. The tripod is, it is thought, of Roman manufacture. NEXT YEAR'S WESLEYAN PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The Pastoral Session of the Wesleyan Conference at York on Thursday, nominated the Rev. William Perkins as president of the Conte:ewe for 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July. 25, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>party but on all Siad3 there hal been a pretty goneral opinion that at the Board of Trade they rezently hal in</p>
        <p>_ MR. LLOYD GEORGE i a man who was in a singular degree a Minister 1 of Commerce—(hear, hear,)—and- he bad heard I that tribute paid most generously to Mr. Lloyd , George quite as much by political opponents 1 as by political supporters. He thought that if they con- sidered the vigilance and the combination of enter- prise and good judgment with which Mr. Buxton had I handled so many things connected with the Post O'H..le, they might say of him that he also in the interests of Britain had beea a Minister of Commerce. (Hear, bear.) It was very important that Mr. Balton had safeguarded our wireless telegraphy and protected us against anv monopoly in that direction. Haar, hear.) The last piece of work to which he vvonl allade was, he thought, very characteristic of the Yostmaster General, and that was the change by which he had, as fares it was passible to do so, brought down pastal charges on all the literature that was needed for the blind in England to a level with the charges for the ordinary postage. (Applause') There was a story of a sculptor who once committed suicide—he could not very well do it twice—(laughter)—because he had carved a statue which was so perfect that he could not find a fault in it. So he said he had nothing left to live for in the way of improvement. Hs (Mr. Croatield) did not know whether they could quite say that yet of their guest and his work. (Laughter.) Ha would perhaps have to stretch a paint if he put the postal service quite so high as that—(laughter)—and it , would be a little bit inconsistent, because he was rash I enough to make a . SUGGESTION IN CONNECTION WITH WARRINGTON the other day in the House, which he was still hoping the Postmaster would take into serious consideration— (hear, hear). But at any rate, if one of the greatest, permanent, and most legitimate sources of pleasure was work well done, he thought their friend Mr. Buxton would hava reason to be one of the happiest men in Eagland to-day. (Applause). Mr.l Buston's courtesy, industry, insight and all round ability were recognised and appreciated in every quarter of the House of Commons and he was sure he could say in the name of all present, as well as on his own aceount, that they in Warrington ware proud indeed to welcome so flue a type of an English statesman. (Applause) The toast was drunk with musical honours.</p>
        <p>MR. BTJXTIAN'S SPEECH. - SUGGESTED PENN POST TO FRANCE. Mr. BUXTON, in responding. said he thanked them very much for the hearty reception they had been good enough to give him and especially for the musical honours, and he thought his special thanks were due to the Mayor for leading the chorus in such an admirable way. (Laughter and applause). It had given him very great pleasure to have the honour of coming down to open the new post ollice in their ancient and respected town—(hear, hear)—and he was glad to know that they had with them their friend Mr. Monks who was able to show what a great deal they had done in postal matters and postal reforms during the last thirty or forty yeirs. I Probably Mr. Monks would not wish him to put it further back than that. (Laughter). Mr. Monks reminded him that in the old days—by which of course he did not mean his days—(laughter)—the postal address in the coaching times for Liverpool was "LIVERPOOL, NEAR WARRINGTON." He (Mr. Buxton) was glad to think it had never degenerated into • Warrington,near Liverpool." He was glad also to have the opportunity of meeting not only the Corporation but the leading manufacturers of the town. They were fortunate in Warrington in one respect in business matters—that they had a consider- able variety of manufactures. (Hear, hear.) They did not depend entirely on 023 industry and that of course had its advantages when there ware variations, as unfortunately there were, in trade. He was glad to recognise in opening the post office, the very public spirited way in which the Corporation and the citizens of Warrington had come forward in regard to the site of. that building. He was happy to think that public spirit was prevalent in England and that it was especially prevalent in Warrington. He was told it dated back some time in Warrington because there seeeraed to be a story of one of their Mayors who when the victory of Waterloo was announced, set fire to his house in order tohave a good bon-fire in honour of Waterloo. (Laughter.) He was sorry to say that in Liverpool they said—of course the name of the Meyer was not Smethurst— (laughter)—that the house was heavily insured. (Laugh- ter.) Continuing Mr. Buxton thanked Mr. Croadeld very warmly for the kind words he had uttered with reference to himself. What pleased him more than anything else was the allusion to the fact that the various</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>POSTAL U.EPOR MS</head>
        <p>POSTAL U.EPOR MS</p>
        <p>----- which he had had the opportunity and advantage of carrying out had been done spontaneously, without public pressure Or public advertisement. (Hear, hear.) Coming, if they would allow him to say so, from a manufacturer of soap, he considered that a real compli- ment. (Laughter.) Of course, he knew that godliness was next to cleanliness and therefore he thought we Englishmen and especially the people of Warrington I might consider that our -opportunities of going to the better of the two places in the future worli were very good, because if there was one thing which was thoroughly well advertised throughout the length and breadth of England, it was soap. (Laughter.) One reform which Mr. Crostield specially commented upon was the agreement he (Mr. Buxton) had lately been able to come to with America with reference to the reduction of the postage between that -country and England, placing it on the same basis as our colonial postage, of a penuy an ounce. (Applause.) He would like to point out that that was really a more material redaction than I appeared on the face of it. Coupled with other postal improvements, such as the increase of weight, it meant that from the first of October next an ounce letter which, last September, would have cost flvepence would only be charged one penny, and a two-ounce letter, which would enable enclosures to be sent and which previously cost tenpence would in future only cost twopence. This change, together with improvements in the parcel post, would be a considerable advantage to business firms in this country trading with America. He agreed with Mr. Crosfleld that apart from the business aspect, these opportunities of greater connection between the citizens of one country and another must in the end have a very material effect on the personal relations between the two countries. (Hear hear.) He was sure no country in these days of civilization was what one might call a war-like country. No nation initially desired to go to war, but excitement arose, personal relations were strained, and war broke out. Those personal relations were less likely to become excited to the eatent of being dangerous if the means of intercourse between the two countries were extended from time to time. fie would say the same with regard to OTHER EXTENSIONS OF TIIE PENNY POSTAGE system to which they were looking in the future. He was sorry to say one could not promise at the present moment, with the finances of the country in a somewhat parlous condition, that any further great extension could be carried out to France or elsewhere, but he was hopeful and he certainly desired that when opportunity occured and when he could persuade the Treasury that the money would be well invested and that he would be able to return it to them before many days—which they seldom believed—(laughter)—he would ,be able to extend the penny postage to France and other Con- tinental nations. (Applause.) He considered it was an advantage that public interest should be evoked in a great department such as that over which he presided because, after all,the Post Office was just as much their business as it was that of the Postmaster General. What the public really wanted to know was whether this great department—an. example, he would not say of Socialism, but of State management—was carried on satisfactorily and efficiently. The public did not know much about the way in which the Post Office was conducted. They elways assumed that their letters, whether properly directed or not, whether written legibly or illegibly, would arrive at their destination, and if they did not the unfortunate Postmaster-General usually heard of it. Those who were responsible for the Post Oalce knew that it required an immense amount of efficient administration , and organisation in order that this great machine should I run smoothly and without friction. The publics some- times complained about them—usually unjustly— (laughter) —in fact he had tried to work it out on mathematical lines, and of</p>
        <p>THE COMPLAINTS jrE RECEIVED , he should say about 821 per cent, were entirely unfounded ! and tho other 17i per cent, were more or less unjust. (Laughter.) He did not like exaggerating, so he would I say that the other 17 per cent. were not always well founded and that left about a half per cent, for which 'there was a certain amount of justification. (Laughter.) j Only yesterday or the day before, he had a furious i 'telegram from someone who wanted know why a letter ; which had been written to him (Mr. Buxton) on post ' Wilco matters had not been acknowledged, and that furious person forgot to put any name or address on the telegram or any possible indication as to where the letter came from. (Laughter). As Postmaster-General he had many thousands of letters a day and he was unable to identify that particular letter or answer the telegram, so he had no doubt that the writer had gone about ever since saying that the Post Office was the I most unbusinesslike affair in the Kingdom and that the , ' Postmaster was the most ineflicent member of the ; Government he or she had over known. (Laughter). ' The Postal Department, however,had grievances against the public—and he also was a member of the public in , his unofficial hours, which were very few. (Laughter). ' Of course, in Warrington he had not any doubt that everything was done perfectly rightly, and he was sure that anybody whom his friend Mr. Crosfield repre- sented must be of the highest possible intelligence. (Laughter). But apart from Warrington they had grievances. The public were always wanting to receive I their letters much too early and to post them much tool late—(laughter)—and the result was that the pressure : on the post office staff was very considerable. Ile would seriously appeal to the public, as the representative of that great department, in regard to such questions as Sundiy labour ad night work, and the provision of a I WEEKLY HALF-HOLIDAY FOR POSTMEN. 1</p>
        <p>• Everyone desired that the half-holiday movement should he extended to the Post OdiceJservice, as far as it could be done without undue inconvenience to the public. In Warrington the public had been good enough to agree to the suspension of a particular delivery on a cortaia day in order to give the post=</p>
