        <p>ying the., __ whilst the more 4 generously disposed would have to pay more than their share. It bad been recognized by the legislature that the feeding of school children was national obligation and anyone who thought about it must realize that a question of such vital importance to the children and to the future of the race was a national obligation and should be borne by the nation and the local authorities. He moved that the scheme be adopted with the exception of the clause relating to the appeal. Mr. H. ROBERTS, in seconding said he was in entire sympathy with the line of argument adopted by Alder- man Bennett. The fact that the State had made this work more or less incumbent upon education authorities gave it legal recognition which it ha i not prior ta the Act being passed. and therefore be did not think the community should be asked to differentiate between what was charity in the broad and comsabnly under- Blood sense, and what was a duty as defined by this Act of Parliament. Mr. PLINSTON said no one was more strongly in support of the principle of the amendment than him- self, but they knew as a fact that they were bound to make an appeal for voluntary help before they could levy a id. rate. Mr. BURGESS : Is there a clause in the Act of Parliament to that effect? The CHAIRMAN : Yes. If the public appeal fails we are entitled to apply to the Board of Education. AlderGIRE BENNETT said that if that was SO it remoued the necessity for the amendment, but it did not inter- fere with the general scope of his contention. Mr. ICrxonox : Am I right in concluding that the line of action that would meet with the sympathy of the proposer and seconder of the amendment would be that we instruct our friends to be careful not to sub- scribe ? (Laughter). The Rev. J. YONGE : They will be careful enough, without any telling. (Laughter). The DIRECTOR said it was not a provision of the Act but an order of the Board of Education that before they could get consent to levy a rate, they must prove that there were no voluntary sources from which they could pbtain the money. Mr. H. ROBERTS : I don't know who the author of that particular method of doing business is,but it seems to me to be about the silliest thing I have come across for some time. I sincerely hope it is note member of my own party. (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN : lam afraid it is. (Laughter). The minutes were then approved. TECHNICAL INSTITUTE EXTENSIONS.</p>
        <p>A letter having been submitted from the Board of Education, oonveying their approval of the plans- for I the extension of the Technical Itistitute,it was resolved that the Surveyor be instructed to prepare the quantities and obtain tenders for the carrying out of the work. Mr. PLINSTCYN said he thought there was an under- standing that prior to any procedure with the work, the committee should have another opportunity of con- sidering the scheme. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH said he was of the same opinion. The DIRECTOR said the understanding was that the extension was not to interfere with any enlargement scheme that might take place on the Museum-street I , site. The plans would not intagere with proposals of that kind. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH : I. certainly thought the plans were coming before the Committee apin before they were carried out. • The CHAIRMAN: They were before this Committee 4ind then they sent up to the Board of Education, so , that there has bean nothing done except that we have I receved their consent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>p-rOC-eedings then closed with the singing of the National Anthem. MR. SIMON AND THE SUFFRAGETTES. A Satisfactory Interview.</head>
        <p>The p-rOC-eedings then closed with the singing of the National Anthem. MR. SIMON AND THE SUFFRAGETTES. A Satisfactory Interview.</p>
        <p>The visit of the Postmaster-General was regarded by the suffragettes as a suitable occasion on which to advance their propaganda. A contingent of eight ladies came over from Manchester' all wearing sashes which bore the war-cry "Votes for Women," their object being to present a petition to Nlr. Buxton. They were posted in several places where it was thought he would pass, each one having a copy of the document ready for immmediate use. The police succeeded in keeping them away from the Postmaster-General, and, after the ceremony of opening, while he was being entertained by Mr. A. 11. Crosfleld, the ladies waited about the various approaches to the hall. While thus waiting they received an intimation from Mr. Buxton that he would receive a deputation within the hall, and two (4rs. Baines, of Stockport, and Bliss Brook, of Huddersfield), were told off to wait upon the right hon. gentleman. They were supplied with a bottle of champagne and a waiter to while away the time till Mr. Buxton was ready for the ordeal. The interview took place in the presence of the Mayor. Afterwards the ladies informed the Press representatives that Mr. Buxton was asked whether he would use his influenoe as a Cabinet Minister to get the Women's Suffrage Bill passed this session. Mr. BUXTON told them that he was in favour of votes for women, and had voted for the Bill. He added that he considered Mr. Asquith had made a good offer in intimating that women's suffrage would probably be , dealt with in a large measure of electoral reform. He said he did not care for the militant suffragists' tacticp, ' especially the breaking up of Cabinet ministers' meet- ings, from which he had personally been a sufferer. The ladies replied that they were only doing their hest to bring their views before the country. If every Cabinet minister dealt with them in the same spirit as had Mr. Buxton, they would never trouble them so far I as their meetings were concerned. "We are very well satisfied" they aided "with our visit to Warrington."</p>
        <p>In the evening the suffragettes held a public meeting at Bridge Foot, where a huge crowd assembled and endeavoured to derive amusement by making jocular interruptions. It appeared to be the general opinion, however, that the ladies were very capable advocates of their cause. The iollowing are the names of the suffragettes who, it may be stated, are connected with the Women's Social and Political Union :—Ura. Baines (Stockport), Miss Brook (Huddersfield), Mrs. Duncan (Disley), Miss Woodlock (Liverpool), Misses Marsden, Capper, Drummond and Robinson (Manchester).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>Almost twenty memgers of the Warrington Club carried out tbe run to Hale on Saturday last, a most enjoyable outing being the result. An excellent tea partaken of at the" Childe "Hotel was followed by a bowling handicap on the picturesque little green adjoining. The handicapping, Szc., was carried out by the Secretary and G. H. Anderton, both of whom, curiously enough, drew byes in the first round which caused smiles to appear on the faces of the members, which considerably broadened when Mr. Andertou helped himself to the first prize.</p>
      </div>
