      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Councillor BAR-I:o—ai 8.6-k;Ci if anything further had beerr done by the Committee with reference to the site for a new school lathe Bank Quay locality. gr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH : We had a mee ting last Wednesday and I think there is a reasonable prospect that we may be able to get a site in a short time. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MEDICAL INSPECTION.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL INSPECTION.</p>
        <p>At a meetini—ol-Cle—Medi—c-af Inspection of School Children's Committee a report to the joint meeting of the Health Committee and this Cammittee, dealing with proposed arrangements for carrying out the redical inspection of school children, was submitted. It was resolved, that the Health Committee be asked</p>
        <p>to state what Officers they purpose providing, and what their duties will be for carrying out the niedical inspection of school be, and what portion of the work they require to be carried out by this Committee. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>COUNTY EDUCATION COMMITTEES.</head>
        <p>COUNTY EDUCATION COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE. At the meeting of the Lancashire Education Com- mittee at Preston on Monday, Sir Henry Hibbert, who presided, was congratulated upon his being recently honoured by the degree of LL.D. from the Victoria University Manchester.</p>
        <p>, The report of the Cotton Industry Sub-Committee having been submitted, Sir Henry drew attention to the recommendation that a cotton-weaving scholarship, awarded in 1906, be renewed for 1908-9, and that the, student be permitted, as requested, to take advantage of the option which allowed a scholar to spend his third year in a mill, providing that on inquiry it was found he. would be given sufficient opportunity of extending and advancing his practical knowledge. Sir Henry said this privilege had been taken advantage of, and !the com- mittee would watch the experiment with considerable interest. He was glad to be able to report that the deputation sent to the Board of Education on the question of increased grants for secondary schools was met very favourably. The net result of the concessions made by the Board was equal to a sum of £1,265. Archdeacon Fletcher presented the report of the. Scholarships Sub-Committee containing the scheme of scholarships and exhibitions to be awarded in 1909, and pointed out that in several respects the scheme differed from its predecessor. With regard to the exhibition for persons intending to become pupil teachers, their proposed that it should be withdrawn. It was proposed to raise the number of junior exhibitions restricted to children in attendance at public elementary schools from 250 to 350, and abolish the special exhibition for candidates intending to become teachers. The cost of the scheme proposed would be £25,800, which included examination and other expenses, as compared with the present scheme, which was estimated to £25,900—a saving of £l,lOO. Mr. Einch seconded the adoption of the report which was agreed to.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE. PROPOSAL POR ORGANISED G IMES.</p>
        <p>• The Cheshire Education Committee at. Craw° cu Monday discussed the question of organised games ill connection with the elementary schools. Dr. Hodgson (who presided) referred to a discussion at the last meeting., when a resolution was moved that no organised games in the future should be paid for out of the rates. He hoped that the Committee would not take such a serious step as to absolutely refuse to carry out the injunction in the Code with regard to organised games. He was strongly against wasteful expenditure, but he thought that money could be advantageously spent in this direction. What was useful and available . . . _</p>
        <p>fin. secondary schools should be equally available for elementary schools. Mr. Roger Bate thought that games were not a material part of education for which they should levy rates.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. R. Bailey said that in rural areas landlords would generally be found willing to give the use of land for games ; but in populous centres that was not possible. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Gleave said Out games wore luxuries, and seeing how their expenditure was increasing, he thought the people would object to pay for games. Colonel Dixon thought that, considering the heavy charges that organised games would be upon the rates. some provision might be made, at any rate in rural districts, by voluntary effort. Mr. J. U. Yates said his experience was that in the towns when the children were not in school they were turned into the streets. Where else were they to go? He thought they should seriously consider the necessity' of playing grounds. After a long discussion the following resolution was adopted :—"That the several administrative sub-com- mittees be urged to stimulate and promote local effort in providing organised games, and that where local effort is unsuccessful in meetiog the want it is still open to the Committee to supplement local effort by any well-considered scheme.'</p>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME. The Palace nlanagernent, ever up to date, have this week presented Warrington audiences with an excep- tional turn in the shape of Salome. Since Maud Allan first startled and fascinated London with her repre- - sentation of the dance of Salome before the head of John the Baptist, and the series of classical dances which aim .at making music visible, people all over the country have shown an anxiety to witness the unique performance. Miss Allan has so 'far not per- - formed in this part of the country, thanks to the _- prudery of, the Manchester Watch Committee, but " there are other exponents of her style of dancing and ; it was introduced to Warrington audiences this week by Miss Helen Roberts, a young lady in her teens, but , with an extensive dancing experience gained in Milan. Miss Roberts has charmed Warrington people nightly by her grace and skill in the Terpsichorean art. Her whole performance breathes of the classical, - and as such it should be above the suspicion which certain critics who have never seen this phase of dancing seem to invest it with. She had three dances. The first was to Mendelssohn's Spring Song, from the Lieder Ohne Worte,in which the dancer's movements,- * possessed of so much charm and grace, are suggestive of waving fields of corn and woodlands in the Spring- ~ time. It is a fine poetic picture, and the way Nlendelssohn's beautiful music was played by the orchestra led by Mr. Widdop, certainly added to the dignity of the dance. The second was to a valse 13:1 Chopin, in which the dancing is of a high order, with a smart example of the toe dance introduced. We were scarcely as much impressed with the Salome dance- as we expected, though it is weird in character and the artiste most cleverly conveys by motion aad gesture the horror which overcome her at the sight of the grisly head. A further touch of realism is given by the Egyptian setting of the scene. As to Miss Roberts' costumes they are scant, yet ample. We see nothing to object to in this aspect of her performance. The same dances by an artiste in the usual stage accoutrements would seem merely silly and purpose- less. A clove? and highly amusing turn is provided by Robins and Collier in their comedy railway station scene,' which will be remembered as one of the most popular features of one of the winter panto - mines. Les Zelovas are a pair of remarkably muscular and expert acrobatic equilibrists and earn many a round of applause. Professor Norton displays admirable sleight of hand, with rather an excess .t)f patter, and the Violas thoroughly mystify the audience as second sight experts. Harry Grey, the Australian ((nugget " causes roars of laughter with his suffragette song, while Ernest Rees occupies the boards with irresistible drollery. Marjory Clark and Percy Hayden have an amusing musical comedy turn "By appointment," in which the piano, pyjamas, and a soda-water syphon play conspicueus parts. The bio-tableaux cap an excellent programme, which fulIT maintains the reputation of the house. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMIIE. Another attractive programme will be submitted next week. The bill is headed by Ferguson and 1 Mack, said to be America's funniest eccentric comedians, introducing their latest absurdity " The Dimple Sisters." The Vyponds, society entertainera will provid3 a capital turn and the other artistes be : -Norah Stockelle, comedienne and dancer ; Nv • E. Whittle, ventriloquist, as President Roosevelt " 00 the field of battle ;' Lew Towers, light comedian ana actor vocalist ; Ryder Slone, the popular characteT, comedian, in his latest success Sing Sing;. - Hampton and Bauman, Darktown entertainers, a• " Down in Kentucky ; " Madge Field, comedienne and dancer. Marriages. DA TNTIra—PORTER—JUIy 20th, at the Parish Church Warrington, by the Rev. Canon Willis, William Daintith to Emma Baines Porter. Death. JOSCPM—JuIy 22, at 21. Bold-street. Warringtom, Annie the beloved wife of Dr. George William JosePlu. Cortege leaves residence at twelve o'clock Saturdal.,.., I Service, Trinity Church. Interment, Warrington Cemetery, one pm. IN MEAIORIAM. HOLLAND.—In tender and loving memory of our de3 - , Willie. who passed away July 23rd, 1907. "Till morning's joy shall end the night of weeping- . From Father Mother and Edith. 1.06 '•••••w. Warrington.-13rinted and published by JonN Waracl7, Co., at The Examiner General Printing Wor Bewsey-street, and published by them at the ea"'d place, and also at The Examiner 0 (Sew, Victorialacy WIDNES -32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, M5-rt,,ly- treet, EaItIMSTOWN; Church-street RoticoS,Y. 23th, 1905.</p>
